#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""autofinish — which idle claude/* worktrees a runner would launch by itself, why the rest would
not, and (armed) the start. E2 of docs/autofinish-design-2026-10-02.md, the dry run and the start
rules; E3, the triggers; E6's half, the /autofinish override.

  autofinish --dry-run [<repo>] [--log]
        one line per claude/* worktree of <repo> (a path, or a repos.json key such as
        `shop/pipeline`), or of every repo in repos.json whose own .claude/workflow.json says
        `"checkpoint": true`:  `<repo> <branch> <verdict>`, the verdict one of
            start (A|B|C|D|E|L|M)
            wait: idle N min
            skip: <veto> [(<detail>)]
        --log also appends each verdict as `<iso> dry <repo> <branch> <verdict>` to
        ~/Library/Logs/claude-weekly/autofinish-<yyyy-mm-dd>.log. One line per verdict, fields
        space separated, the verdict last, so `grep ' skip: busy'` or `awk '{print $5}'` read it.
  autofinish --sweep
        what launchd runs every 10 min (bootstrap.sh --schedule, <label-prefix>.autofinish):
        the all-repos dry run, always logged. Armed, it then starts at most SWEEP_CAP `start`
        verdicts, each re-judged just before (R7, with a fetch: see below) and never a tip it
        started before, and logs
          <iso> start <repo> <branch> <rule>
          <iso> failed <repo> <branch> <rule> (<error>)
        Only a start counts against the cap and is remembered: a failed start is tried again at
        the next tick, and one worktree that keeps failing does not starve the rest.
        First, armed or not, every worktree whose run is dead — started, no outcome, its claude
        and watcher gone (bin/autofinish-run's dead()) — is recorded `died` by the runner's
        bury(), which logs `<iso> run <repo> <branch> died session=… tip=… rule=… by=… account=… — …`, and
        printed here as `died <repo> <branch> — <why>`. The dry run reports such a worktree as
        `skip: died, not recorded yet (…)` and writes nothing.
        Prints only those lines (the launchd log); the verdicts are in the dated log.
  autofinish --worktree <path> [--after <s>] [--session <id>] [--reply <file>] [--dry-run] [--log]
        one worktree: its verdict line, then the start on that same verdict, when armed and not
        --dry-run. It is judged with a fetch when a start may follow, and not judged twice: the
        start comes seconds after it, not the sweep's minutes (code review F9). --after waits that long first (SessionEnd's trigger: 60 s; under a pinned
        AUTOFINISH_NOW the clock moves instead of sleeping). --session: the caller's own session
        is at its turn's end, so it is never `busy` for itself (nor is any session among this
        process's ancestors). --reply: the last reply's text from this file, not the transcript —
        the Stop hook's, which has it before the transcript may.
  autofinish arm [--by <who>] | disarm | status
        this Mac's switch, ~/.local/state/autofinish/armed (JSON {"by", "ts"}). Disarmed is the
        default and means: verdicts and logs only, nothing starts, whatever the trigger. The owner,
        2026-10-05: three hours of the dry log first, read by the parent, then armed. `arm`
        refuses under CLAUDE_UNATTENDED (a runner, any dispatch session): an interactive session
        arms on the owner's word; nothing unattended arms itself.
  autofinish override [now | off] [<branch>]
        /autofinish's verb (skills/autofinish): writes or removes the marker for this worktree,
        or for <branch>'s in this repo, then prints the branch's verdict and the switch. Refused
        under CLAUDE_UNATTENDED, and inside a Claude session (CLAUDECODE) unless the last prompt
        queued in this checkout's <git dir>/claude/prompts.json starts with `/autofinish`: the
        marker says `by: <owner>`, so only the owner's typed /autofinish may write it, never a model
        that decided to. A full `claude/*` <branch> no worktree of this repo is on yet is the yes at
        the brief (autofinish-exclude S7, D2-C): the marker waits in the repo's
        <common git dir>/claude/autofinish-briefs/ until a worktree carries that branch, which is then
        judged by rule C and started `--by brief`; `now` is refused for one. The yes predates the
        diff, so the landing re-judges it (brief_held: autofinish-run's landing(), bin/pr): class 2,
        the base's `autofinish_leaks`, and the list itself hold it at a ready PR; class 1 and 3 land.

Read-only but for: --log and --sweep (the log), arm/disarm, override (its marker), the state dir
(the started tips, the sweep lock), a rule-D start's `memo stand --apply` (one commit on that
branch), and an armed sweep's burial of a dead run (bin/autofinish-run's bury: its state, lock,
record and one PR comment), and the judging a start follows: its fresh `account pick` probes
and writes bin/account's cache. No other write to a repo's branches or locks, no session messaged. Origin's tip is read
with `git ls-remote`; GitHub through hooks/common.py's REST client (no `gh`). No fetch while
judging — the dry run and the sweep's pass give `memo status --no-fetch` — except the judging a
start follows (the sweep's re-judge, or --worktree when armed): there memo status fetches, so a
GitHub web edit by the owner voids a stand before anything starts. `start_runner()` calls E1's
bin/autofinish-run.

The run record — the contract E1's runner writes and the hooks read (hooks/common.py's readers):
  <git dir>/claude/autofinish.json, one per worktree, the last run on it:
    {"started": iso, "session": uuid, "tip": sha, "by": "sweep|override|brief|typed|yolo|dnd",
     "rule": "A|B|C|D|E|L|M", "window": str, "account": label|dir|"oauth-token"|null, "ended": iso|null,
     "outcome": "launched|stopped|never-started|died"|null, "pr": int|null, "gate": str|null}
    plus `"merge": true` for a run started with `--merge`, `"hold": "<why>"` for one held at a ready
    PR (S3b; autofinish-run's header); each absent otherwise
    (`launched` was `landed` before the rename's S4, 2026-10-19; every reader takes both)
  Written before the runner's first call (owner.json then names `session`), `ended` and
  `outcome` at its end. While `ended` is null and the lock names `session`, checkpoint.py tells
  a prompt in the old panel once that the run is under way, and refuses its writes with the
  same words (lock_reason); after the end it says the outcome once.
  <common git dir>/claude/autofinished, one line appended per ended run (design section 7):
    <iso> launched <branch> #<pr>   (`landed` before 2026-10-19; no `#<pr>` when the runner could not read the number)
    <iso> stopped <branch> <gate> <the first ⚠ line>
    <iso> never-started <branch> <the first line it kept: the screen's, or dispatch's log>
    <iso> died <branch> <tip7> <dispatch log's last line>   (the sweep's, for a runner that died)
  sync.py's session start says each new line once; `bin/repos --branches` groups the latest per
  branch as `autofinished` and `stopped` (never-started and died are stops).
The state dir (AUTOFINISH_STATE_DIR, else ~/.local/state/autofinish): `armed`; `first-armed`
({"ts"}, the launch door's start, kept across disarm and re-arm); `tried.jsonl`,
one {"ts","repo","branch","tip","rule","kind"} per runner started, so a tip is never started
twice — unless its run record says gate `refused` (`not-launched` before 2026-10-17; dispatch refused
before any session existed: nothing ran); `sweep.lock/`, held while an armed sweep or a --worktree start runs (R12),
taken over after LOCK_TTL, removed only by the pid that holds it.

The start rules (design section 15; D1, D6, D7, D11; rule E: autofinish-widen-2026-10-06 D2-C,
D3-C), checked in this order, first veto wins:
  off       `autofinish` from `git show origin/<main>:.claude/workflow.json` — the base's copy,
            so a branch cannot widen its own list (R8); with none there, the repo's `workflow` in
            the overlay's repos.json; off beside .claude/no-workflow. "records" | "small" | "all" |
            "off"; a missing key is "records" when `records` is set, else "off", and an
            unknown value is "off". Off skips everything but the override.
  state     the worktree on disk; no /move marker; no live session on the branch whose registry
            status is anything but `idle` — busy, waiting on a dialog, a shell (always
            `skip: busy`, override or not); no `finishing` marker under 2 h old; no
            rebase or merge in progress; nothing uncommitted (the checkpoint hook commits every
            turn, so a dirty idle worktree is the owner editing by hand).
  launched  hooks/branches.py's proof → `skip: launched`; its hint → `skip: pickup review` (R10).
  stopped   the worktree's last run (autofinish.json) stopped or never started (gate other than
            `refused`), and nobody has spoken to the branch since it ended: no prompts.json
            entry from another session, no turn's end (last-reply), no override marker newer
            than `ended` → `skip: stopped at <gate> (<ended>) — waits for a prompt`. Without it
            a stop whose own session committed would start again on the new tip (review F6).
  died      the run died (outcome `died`, bin/autofinish-run's bury) is no stop: it is judged once
            more, as below (docs/autofinish-died-2026-10-07.md). The worktree's last two
            ended runs in `autofinished` both `died` on the same tip, and nobody spoke since →
            `skip: died twice (<date>) — waits for a prompt`: a retry that dies too is not a power
            cut but a pattern. A death whose runner had already rewritten the local branch (its
            recorded tip no ancestor of HEAD: curated, then never pushed) → `skip: died
            mid-curation (…) — waits for a prompt`: a /finish on it fails its own lease check.
            A run not yet recorded is `skip: died, not recorded yet (…)` — an armed sweep's first
            pass records it; a disarmed one only says so.
  asks      the diff against the merge-base with origin/<main> adds no `➤ Answer:`, D-item
            (`**D<n> —`), `**Decide:**`, `- [ ]` or `docs/handoff/` line, and no file under
            docs/handoff/ (a seed). Lines are read in .md/.txt files only, and code spans
            don't count: in code the markers are fixtures, in a code span prose about the shape.
            Paths come from `--name-status -z`, the added lines from each path's own diff; a
            line that can't be placed is `skip: asks (<path>: unparsed diff)`, never a pass.
            Override or not (R1). The one exception is rule D.
  D         the branch ADDS one or more D-list docs and every asks line is a `➤ Answer:` or
            D-item line in one of them: `bin/keel status --json <doc>` per doc decides, and D
            starts only when every doc reads `due`, `answered`, or `stood` with its
            `Stood-By: autofinish` commit on the branch. The start runs `memo stand --apply` on
            each `due` doc in the worktree first; an apply that fails is a failed start. A
            `part-stood` doc, or a `due` one whose `unstandable` list is not empty (a stand takes
            only `Reversible: yes` items with a bare pick: entry S3), is a skip — no branch launches
            with `no` or unmarked items open.
            Anything else names the doc and the verdict under `skip: asks`; memo missing or
            failing is `skip: memo status unavailable`, never a start. The rest of the branch
            must be records, text.
  M         (skipper-modes S3b, the landing ladder) the branch's mode — landing_mode: helmlib's
            branch_mode, the plan task naming the branch, else its newest dispatch record — is `dnd`
            or `yolo`, and no override: asks lines only in D-list docs the branch adds, each
            `answered`, `stood` or `taken` (mode_asks: keel yolo's picks no veto here); then any
            shape, size and host, no launch door, never a binary, an `open item` tip, a deploy
            with no `taken` doc (rule L's, which launches it at once), the week's pause (a deploy
            with one: M at once, as L), a busy session, a reply that asks, the idle clock or the
            GitHub gates. Gates are not skipped: autofinish-run's landing() reads the
            mode again and holds at a ready PR what held_slice names — under dnd money in the
            prose added, a physical repo, `autofinish_exclude` and `in_place`, a path the public
            twin's allowlist publishes, a deleted data file, a yolo-taken answer; under both the
            launcher's own GATES. A branch whose mode is `ask` is judged by the rules here and
            every start of it holds (`--hold`). No mode (no sent task; a session the owner types in):
            the rules here, unchanged.
  tip       the tip's message carries no "open item".
  steps     no brief the branch added (docs/handoff/) names a step its Course doc has open —
            `keel landing`, entry S7: `skip: open step (<brief> names <doc> S3 — not started)`.
  L         a commit on the branch (any, not only the tip) carries a `Deployed:` line that says
            something went live (common.deployed: not `no`, `none`, `n/a`, a dash, nor a future
            `on merge`, `after …`, `once …`): the live system runs this branch, so it launches at
            once, the drift being the risk (the owner, 2026-10-13: "If the code is deployed already, it
            should be launched ASAP - no wait"). Any shape and size, any host, the week's pause
            lifted, and a `/autofinish` marker does not bring the idle wait back; still never a
            binary, a gate (below), an `off` repo, a busy session, an ask, a reply that asks, or
            anything the GitHub check refuses. Its idle is none: the Stop hook of a turn whose own
            commit says `Deployed:` pushes at once and hands the branch to the launcher (checkpoint.py,
            autofinish_at_stop), else the next sweep takes it. Only deploys committed from
            common.L_SINCE (2026-10-14) count: an older one keeps `skip: deployed before rule L`,
            the typed /finish it always waited for. Its launches never open E's door (below).
            The cost, known: a sweep may take the worktree between two turns of a live
            conversation, and the next prompt there is refused writes until the run ends.
  shape     A: every changed path under a `records` prefix and `.md`/`.txt` (R13), no binary.
            B ("small" and "all" repos, where A's shape fails): text, under 100 inserted lines,
            on the laptop. Lifted by the override (D7).
            E ("all" repos only, where A's and B's shape fail): text of any size, on the laptop.
            A small diff in an "all" repo stays B. Every other gate holds, the binary veto included.
            The launch door (D1-A: "widen after ~5 real launches", built in so no one has to
            remember it): E, and B in a repo that was not "small" or "all" at the first arm (read
            from origin/<main>'s history; item 2; a mode that cannot be read waits too, and before
            any arm a "small" repo keeps its B), start only once E_LAUNCHES runs the sweep
            started have launched since the first arm — the `launched … by=sweep` end lines (`landed`
            before rename S4, both read), rule L's apart, (common.RUN_LAUNCHED) in the dated logs, not `autofinished`, which the owner's own
            /finish and /autofinish runs fill too. The first arm is `first-armed` in the state
            dir, which arm and disarm keep, so a re-arm does not reset the count; else `armed`'s
            ts. Before that `skip: <E|B> waits for 5 launches (n/5)`, or `(disarmed)`.
            A D-list doc the owner answered in full, beside code in an "all" repo, is no
            ask: the branch is judged as B or E; any other memo verdict there is `skip: asks
            (<doc>: memo <v>, beside code …)`.
  gates     A, B, D, E and L never start a branch touching a gate: in the launcher's repo only, the launcher's
            own files (`hooks/`, `bin/autofinish*`, `skills/finish/`, `skills/autofinish/`,
            `bin/review-gate`, `bin/pr`, `bin/keel`, `bin/account`, `repos.json`,
            `.claude/workflow.json`, `bootstrap.sh`, the git-hook template; GATES), and in every
            repo its `in_place` paths and the prefixes in the base's `autofinish_exclude`
            (workflow.json or the repos.json overlay; either not a list → the repo reads off) →
            `skip: touches a gate (<path>) — launches by /finish or /autofinish only`. The owner's D1-A
            (2026-10-13) lifted the rest — `.github/`, `.claude/` elsewhere, AGENTS.md /
            CLAUDE.md, repo-level git hooks, the check line's scripts — for CI and /finish's own
            gates to hold; so the runner no longer pre-approves the check command. Prefixes, on
            the --no-renames diff, so a gate moved away counts. C (the owner's override) launches a gate.
            Checked before B's and E's host and door. Gates are judged on the tip before the
            start; a run's own later edits are not re-judged (open: docs/autofinish-widen-2026-10-06.md).
  week      the week of the login the runner would start on — `bin/account pick --repo <repo>`:
            the work login first in the walk unless the repo is on its deny list (work-account E5;
            joined early on the owner's word, 2026-10-08 — the sweep had paused all night on the primary login's
            80 % while the work login sat at 6 %). A secondary's week is its registry ceiling, which the
            pick applies; on the primary, that row's `limits --cached` and review-gate's threshold
            (10 points over pace, or 80 % used) pause A, B and E (design section 6, R6; R4 of the widen doc), as
            `skip: week tight (<label> <n>% used, pace <p>%)`. No registry (pick's exit 4), an
            unreadable answer, or a primary with no reading: tier_check's reading of the default
            login, as before. Not C (D7), D or L. The pick reads cached readings (`--cached`)
            except in the judging a start follows. No row may run → `skip: no account (<label>:
            <why>; …)`, for A, B and E here and for every rule just before a start (C and D too: the
            runner would refuse it, and D only after its stand commit).
  reply     the owning session's last reply does not end in `?` nor in the WAITING line
            (common.ending); unread → skip, never a guess.
  idle      40 min (D2) since <git dir>/claude/last-reply (the Stop hook writes it, with the
            reply's tail), else the newest of prompts.json and the owning session's transcript,
            else the tip's commit time. Under the override, a last reply that is the declared
            line (the declared closing line (common.DECLARED_LINES), E6) starts at once, and so
            does any reply under `/autofinish now` (rule C: "at the next turn end whose reply
            asks nothing"). D waits on its deadline, which memo status computed, not on idle.
  GitHub    pushed (origin's tip is ours; origin ahead or apart → `skip: moved on origin`,
            R7), the PR still `WIP:`, and no comment or review on it since the tip's commit.
            After a death, origin at the dead run's recorded tip (local rewritten by it is the
            mid-curation skip above), or descendants of it each carrying that session's
            `Claude-Session:` trailer (a push not yet seen), is not "moved"; the death's own PR
            comment is no activity, anyone else's since the tip still is. A death's tip is started
            again once (`tried` and bin/autofinish-run's same-tip refusal both let it through).

The override (rule C): <git dir>/claude/autofinish, JSON {"by": <the owner, lower-cased>, "ts": epoch, "now": bool},
written by E6's /autofinish (`autofinish override`). It lifts off, shape, size, host
and the week — never busy, asks, reply or GitHub; idle only for the declared line or `now`.

The owning session: owner.json's, else the last prompts.json entry's, else the tip's
`Claude-Session:` trailer (8 hex), else a live registry session whose cwd is the worktree.
Its transcript: ~/.claude/projects/*/<id>.jsonl (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR moves ~/.claude).

Test seams, all environment: AUTOFINISH_NOW (epoch; also pins tier_check's clock),
AUTOFINISH_HOST, AUTOFINISH_REPOS (repos.json), AUTOFINISH_PROJECTS (its root),
AUTOFINISH_MEMO (bin/keel), AUTOFINISH_GITHUB (a JSON fixture {branch: {"pr": {...}|null,
"activity": n}} in place of GitHub), AUTOFINISH_LOG_DIR, AUTOFINISH_STATE_DIR,
AUTOFINISH_RUNNER (a command run as `<it> <worktree> <rule>` in place of start_runner),
AUTOFINISH_ACCOUNT (bin/account; the tests run the real one on ACCOUNTS_FILE and ACCOUNT_NOW),
AUTOFINISH_TEST_BEFORE_START (a shell line run just before the sweep's re-judging, R7's test);
tier_check's own CLAUDE_TIER_CLAUDE_JSON / CLAUDE_TIER_APP_HISTORY. Tests: bin/test_autofinish.sh.
Python 3.9, stdlib only.
"""
import argparse
import datetime
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import urllib.parse
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
from pathlib import Path

BIN = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(BIN.parent / "lib"))
import core_path  # noqa: E402 — the regatta core (C2); none: one line, exit 2
core_path.require()
import common  # noqa: E402 — host, GitHub REST, the lock's reader, the closing lines
# branches.py is wharf's hook (a hard edge, M2): its folder joins the path, after the core's.
_BRANCHES = common.tool("branches.py")
if not _BRANCHES:
    sys.exit("autofinish: wharf's hooks/branches.py not found — install the `wharf` module beside wharf-runs")
sys.path.insert(2, str(_BRANCHES.parent))
import branches  # noqa: E402 — launched / probably launched: only it judges that

CONFIG = Path(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or Path.home() / ".claude")
PROJECTS = CONFIG / "projects"
REGISTRY = CONFIG / "sessions"
# The registry: AUTOFINISH_REPOS for the tests, else the core's one reader (C3). A Mac with none reads
# a path that does not exist, so every repo reads off.
REPOS_JSON = Path(os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_REPOS") or common.repos_json() or "/nonexistent/repos.json")
ROOT = Path(os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_PROJECTS") or Path.home() / "projects")
# keel (M4) and account (lighthouse) are peers found through the core; absent, the path below does
# not exist, the call raises OSError and its caller's own fallback holds (memo_status None, no pick).
MEMO = os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_MEMO") or str(common.tool("keel") or BIN / "keel")
LOG_DIR = Path(os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_LOG_DIR") or Path.home() / "Library/Logs/claude-weekly")

IDLE_MIN = 40          # D2: "the owner is done" (40 min, decoupled from the lock)
SMALL_LINES = 100      # rule B: under this many inserted lines
E_LAUNCHES = 5         # the launch door: E (and B where no B ran at the first arm) after this many sweep launches since the first arm (widen doc D1-A, D2-C, item 2)
# The launcher's own gates (path prefixes), in the launcher's repo only: B, D and E never start a branch that
# touches one. The owner's D1-A (docs/autofinish-everything-2026-10-11.md, 2026-10-13) lifted every
# other gate — .github/, .claude/, AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md, the repo-level git hooks, the check
# line's scripts — in every repo: "secure it through tests on CI, not by slowing down
# development"; /finish still runs every gate, and the base's workflow.json, never the branch's,
# is what this reads (R8). What stays is what the widen doc's item 3 named first: hooks/, which
# every session runs, and the launcher, which must not widen itself unwatched (bin/autofinish*,
# skills/finish/, skills/autofinish/, bin/review-gate, bin/pr, the config it reads — repos.json and
# the overlay's own .claude/workflow.json, whose records or merge key would widen it — bootstrap.sh,
# which decides which hooks every account runs, bin/keel and bin/account, which decide rule D and
# the week gate, and the git-hook template; code review, 2026-10-13). A repo adds its own with `autofinish_exclude`.
GATES = ("hooks/", "bin/autofinish", "skills/finish/", "skills/autofinish/", "bin/review-gate",
         "bin/pr", "bin/keel", "bin/memo", "bin/account", "repos.json", ".claude/workflow.json", "bootstrap.sh",
         "templates/code/git-hooks/")
# the repo GATES apply to, by folder name; AUTOFINISH_LANDER, the old name of AUTOFINISH_LAUNCHER, is read until S8
# Default: the overlay checkout's folder name (overlay_root), none where no overlay is installed, so no repo is the launcher's.
LAUNCHER = os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_LAUNCHER") or os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_LANDER") or (common.overlay_root().name if common.overlay_root() else None)
OWNER = common.OWNER_NAME or "the owner"   # who the verdicts and refusals say is waited on
OWNER_S = OWNER + "'s"
LAPTOP = common.LAPTOP  # rule B runs on the laptop only (D3, D6)
FINISHING_TTL = 2 * 3600  # checkpoint.py's: an older `finishing` marker is an abandoned /finish
GIT_BUSY = ("rebase-merge", "rebase-apply", "MERGE_HEAD", "CHERRY_PICK_HEAD", "REVERT_HEAD")  # checkpoint.py's BUSY_MARKERS
TEXT_SUFFIXES = (".md", ".txt")  # R13: a records prefix can gain a file a script reads
DECLARED = common.DECLARED_LINES  # in common.CLOSING since E6; the old line and the new (rename S2)
MEMO_TIMEOUT = 30
# dry_run() judges a repo's worktrees this many at a time. A verdict is ~20 git processes, plus
# bin/keel, ps, `gh auth token` and up to 4 GitHub REST reads, and a temporary index (read-tree)
# in branches.probably; nothing it writes is shared, and no start (the starts come after, one at
# a time, each judged again). A GitHub refusal under 8 at once reads `skip: GitHub unreachable`,
# never a start. The wall time was git's start-up, serially: 37 s for the suite's 75-worktree repo on a loaded Mac, 11 s at 8
# (2026-10-17). The per-process caches a verdict fills take _CACHE to fill, and are read without
# it once filled: each is assigned whole, so a thread sees nothing or all of it.
JUDGE_THREADS = 8
_CACHE = threading.RLock()  # reentrant: week() reads tier_check(), each filling its own cache
STATE = common.autofinish_state_dir()
# Starts per sweep. The design names the cap (section 15, "the sweep's cap") but no number; one
# is the brief's (2026-10-05): a runner is a whole /finish session, and the next tick is 10 min away.
SWEEP_CAP = 1
LOCK_TTL = 20 * 60  # a sweep lock older than this is a sweep that died: taken over (R12)
RUNNER_TIMEOUT = 120  # start_runner's wait: autofinish-run detaches after its pick (15 + 5 s) and ending a warm run (90 s), 110 s at worst
TAIL_BYTES = 4_000_000  # a transcript's tail read for the last reply; tool output runs to MBs
REFUSED_GATES = common.REFUSED_GATES  # bin/autofinish-run's gate for a run dispatch refused: nothing ran
STOOD_BY = "Stood-By: autofinish"  # bin/keel's STOOD_TRAILER, on the commit `stand --apply` makes
DIED = "died"  # bin/autofinish-run's outcome for a runner that ended without one (its bury())
DIED_HEAD = "**autofinish runner died**"  # bin/autofinish-run's death comment: no one speaking

CODE_SPAN = re.compile(r"`[^`]*`")
D_ITEM = re.compile(r"\*\*D\d+\s*[—–-]")      # `**D1 —**` and `**D1 — Title.**` alike
DECIDE = re.compile(r"\*\*Decide:\*\*")
CHECKBOX = re.compile(r"^\s*[-*+]\s+\[ \]")
HANDOFF = branches.HANDOFF_DIR + "/"
LINK_ONLY = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:[-*]\s*)?(?:<?https?://\S+>?|\[[^\]]*\]\([^)]*\))\s*$")


def now():
    return float(os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_NOW") or time.time())


def host():
    return os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_HOST") or common.host_name()


def git(repo, *args, timeout=30):
    """stdout stripped, or None when git failed — never an exception to the caller."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None


# ---- the seam E1 fills --------------------------------------------------------------------

def runner_by(worktree, rule):
    """`--by` for a start: `override` for rule C, else `sweep` — but `yolo` when the worktree's last
    run was a yolo launch that died (E12, review of 2026-10-08): its retry must stay one, or a plain
    `/finish --unattended` would merge the taken answers where the yolo run stops at a held PR."""
    gitdir = git(worktree, "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir")
    run = common.autofinish_run(Path(gitdir) / "claude") if gitdir else None
    if rule == "M":
        # rule M's start names its mode (S3b); autofinish-run reads plan.json again before it merges
        # (a mode lowered since the verdict: `sweep`, and autofinish-run holds a rule-M start whatever its --by)
        # with the repo, as verdict reads it: a same-named branch in a sibling repo has its own mode
        cdir = git(worktree, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
        primary = str(Path(cdir).parent) if cdir and cdir.endswith("/.git") else None
        m = landing_mode(git(worktree, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"), primary)[0]
        return m if m in LAND_MODES else "sweep"
    if run and run.get("by") in ("yolo", "dnd") and run.get("outcome") == DIED:
        return run["by"]
    if rule == "C":
        # `override` — the owner's full yes — only while the worktree's own marker reads; anything else
        # (the yes at the brief, S7, a marker turned off since the verdict, a git timeout) starts `brief`,
        # which the landing re-judges by brief_held: never more than was given (landing review, finding 10)
        return "override" if gitdir and override(Path(gitdir) / "claude") else "brief"
    return "sweep"


def start_runner(worktree, rule, cfg=None):
    """Start `/finish <branch> --unattended` in `worktree` through bin/autofinish-run (E1), which
    pre-writes the lock, detaches and returns at once. Rule C is the owner's /autofinish, so it is
    recorded as `override`; every other rule is the sweep's. A non-zero exit is a failed start
    (2: not a claude/* worktree, 3: refused — that tip tried, or a run already going)."""
    by = runner_by(worktree, rule)
    # without the login variables a hook or a dispatched session passed down: the runner picks
    # its login as week() did; only a typed hand-off (cold_prompt) keeps the caller's. `cfg`: the
    # dir attempt()'s fresh pick chose, passed as an explicit login so the runner starts on the
    # row the gate checked, not a second pick a minute later (review, 2026-10-08); "" is the
    # default login, which autofinish-run reads back as unset
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k not in LOGIN_ENV}
    if cfg is not None:
        env["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = cfg or str(Path.home() / ".claude")
    r = subprocess.run([str(BIN / "autofinish-run"), str(worktree), "--rule", rule, "--by", by],
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=RUNNER_TIMEOUT, env=env)
    if r.returncode:
        raise RuntimeError((r.stderr.strip() or f"exit {r.returncode}")[:120])


# ---- inputs -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def repo_config(repo, main, at=None):
    """(mode, records, problem, exclude) from origin/<main>:.claude/workflow.json — the base's
    copy, never the branch's (R8) — else the repo's `workflow` in the overlay's repos.json; off beside
    a .claude/no-workflow on origin/<main>. `exclude`: its `autofinish_exclude` and `in_place`
    prefixes, gated beside GATES. `at`: the same read at that commit of it (the launch door's "what was it at the
    first arm"), never the overlay, which only knows today."""
    ref = at or f"origin/{main}"
    if git(repo, "show", f"{ref}:.claude/no-workflow") is not None:
        return "off", [], f"origin/{main} carries .claude/no-workflow", []
    text = git(repo, "show", f"{ref}:.claude/workflow.json")
    if text is None and at is not None:
        # repos.json today is not what it was at `at`: reading it let a repo's flip to "all"
        # skip the launch door (review, 2026-10-11)
        return "off", [], f"no .claude/workflow.json at {at[:12]}; the overlay is today's", []
    if text is None:
        # a repo that keeps its opt-in out of git: the overlay's repos.json, which the branch
        # under judgement cannot edit either (common.workflow_overlay)
        cfg = common.workflow_overlay(repo, REPOS_JSON)
        if not isinstance(cfg, dict):
            return "off", [], f"no .claude/workflow.json on origin/{main}, none in repos.json", []
    else:
        try:
            cfg = json.loads(text)
        except ValueError:
            return "off", [], "workflow.json on origin is not JSON", []
    records = [r for r in cfg.get("records") or [] if isinstance(r, str) and r]
    raw_ex = cfg.get("autofinish_exclude")
    if raw_ex is not None and not isinstance(raw_ex, list):  # a safety list must not fail open
        return "off", records, f"autofinish_exclude is not a list ({type(raw_ex).__name__})", []
    exclude = []
    # in-place paths never launch from a branch (knowledge-commit refuses them): gated in every repo,
    # so a repo need not copy its `in_place` into `autofinish_exclude` (review, 2026-10-13)
    raw_ip = cfg.get("in_place")
    if raw_ip is not None and not isinstance(raw_ip, list):  # a safety list must not fail open
        return "off", records, f"in_place is not a list ({type(raw_ip).__name__})", []
    inplace = raw_ip or []
    for p in (raw_ex if isinstance(raw_ex, list) else []) + inplace:
        p = (p[2:] if p.startswith("./") else p.lstrip("/")) if isinstance(p, str) else ""
        if p:  # an empty prefix would gate every path: a typo, not a wish
            exclude.append(p)
    mode = cfg.get("autofinish") or ("records" if records else "off")
    if mode not in ("records", "small", "all", "off"):
        return "off", records, f"unknown autofinish value {mode!r}", exclude
    return mode, records, None, exclude


def touches_gate(repo, main, exclude, paths):
    """The first of `paths` under one of the repo's gates — GATES in the launcher's own repo, the
    repo's `autofinish_exclude` everywhere — as plain prefixes (`bin/autofinish` is
    bin/autofinish-run too), or None. The diff runs --no-renames, so a gate moved away shows
    under its old path."""
    gates = (tuple(GATES) if Path(repo).name == LAUNCHER else ()) + tuple(exclude)
    return next((p for p in sorted(paths) if gates and p.startswith(gates)), None)


def gate_skip(repo, main, exclude, paths):
    """touches_gate's verdict line, or None — the one wording for rules A, B, D, E and L."""
    gate = touches_gate(repo, main, exclude, paths)
    return f"skip: touches a gate ({gate}) — launches by /finish or /autofinish only" if gate else None


# ---- the landing ladder (skipper-modes S3b) -----------------------------------------------
# The owner, 2026-10-21: "dnd: I want to automerge as well. apply the same logic to merges". A sent task's
# mode (helmlib, plan.json the authority) decides how far its branch lands by itself: `ask` never (every
# launch holds at a ready PR: autofinish-run adds --hold), `default` by the rules above, `dnd` every
# branch of the ship bar the held slice (held_slice), `yolo` whatever passes the check and the review
# gate bar the launcher's gates and the repo's `autofinish_exclude` and `in_place` (the landing review). Rule M is the sweep's start for a dnd or yolo branch: any shape, any host, `taken` docs
# included, a deploy only with one (without, rule L launches it at once); never a binary, an open item, the week's pause, a busy session or the GitHub gates. Whether
# that start merges or holds is autofinish-run's, which reads the mode again itself (never the sweep's
# word, nor a caller's --by): a mode only the owner raises (helmlib.raise_refusal) is the yes to merge.
LAND_MODES = ("dnd", "yolo")
# keel's DND_HOLDS money pattern, over the prose a branch adds (.md/.txt, code spans cut): a model cost
# written `$<list> ($<eff> effective)` is no money (rules: every model cost), nor a bare $0 in a table
MONEY_RE = re.compile(r"[$€£]\s?(?!0(?![\d.,]))\d|\blist-only\b|\b(?:usd|eur|gbp)\b|\bsubscription\b|\binvoice\b", re.I)
MODEL_COST_RE = re.compile(r"\$[\d.,]+[kKmM]?\s*\(\s*\$[\d.,]+[kKmM]?\s+effective\s*\)")
DATA_SUFFIXES = (".json", ".jsonl", ".ndjson", ".csv", ".tsv", ".yaml", ".yml", ".sqlite", ".db")
YOLO_TAKEN = "Taken unattended (%s's yolo, " % (common.OWNER_NAME or "Owner")   # keel's YOLO_ANSWER (its owner key, "Owner" unset): no holds were applied to it
PHYSICAL_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*-\s*physical:\s*(.*?)\s*$", re.M)


def landing_mode(branch, repo=None):
    """(mode, where) of the newest sent task on `branch` — helmlib.branch_mode: the plan task naming
    it, else its newest run record — or (None, why) for a branch no task was sent on (a session the owner
    types in has no mode) and for a mode that cannot be read, which lands by the rules above."""
    p = common.tool("helmlib.py")
    if not p:
        return None, "helm is not installed here"
    if str(p.parent) not in sys.path:
        sys.path.insert(0, str(p.parent))
    try:
        import helmlib  # noqa: E402 — helm's, a soft edge: absent, no branch has a mode
        return helmlib.branch_mode(branch, str(repo) if repo else None)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — unreadable is no mode, never a crashed sweep
        return None, f"the mode is unreadable ({type(e).__name__})"


def mode_unreadable(why):
    """True when landing_mode's `why` says the mode could not be read (helm absent, a reader that
    raised) — a hold, never "no sent task" (held_slice's rule: what it cannot read is a hold)."""
    return str(why).startswith("the mode is unreadable") or why == "helm is not installed here"


def effective_mode(by, mode, standing_yes=False, recorded=False, unreadable=False):
    """The mode a mode or ship launch is judged by (held_slice's `mode`) — one rule for autofinish-run's
    landing() at the start and bin/pr's re-check at ready and merge (follow-ups' landing review, item 8).
    MODE is the branch's mode now (landing_mode), None for none.
      - unreadable: None, a hold;
      - a ship launch (the keel's yes): a dnd, yolo or ask mode is the mode; a ship with no landing mode —
        branch_mode reads its task `default`, never None — is judged `yolo` at the start, the yes having
        been given to a blueprint (S6's finish review), and at the re-check by what the start recorded;
      - any other: a dnd or yolo mode is the mode; none at all is E12's standing yes (`yolo`) at the start
        when STANDING_YES (a `--by yolo` from a person in a `"merge": "auto"` repo; landing review 3, 4c),
        and at the re-check what the start recorded; any other mode is itself, which holds.
    RECORDED: False at the start; at the re-check the start's `eff_mode` (None for a record from before
    the key, which holds) — never a bare `by: yolo`, which any caller may type (follow-ups item 4)."""
    if unreadable:
        return None
    if by == "ship":
        if mode in LAND_MODES or mode == "ask":
            return mode
        return "yolo" if recorded is False else recorded
    if mode in LAND_MODES:
        return mode
    if mode is None:
        if recorded is not False:
            return recorded
        return "yolo" if by == "yolo" and standing_yes else None
    return mode


def physical_line(repo, main):
    """The contract's `physical:` value on origin/<main> (AGENTS.md, else CLAUDE.md), or None."""
    for name in ("AGENTS.md", "CLAUDE.md"):
        m = PHYSICAL_RE.search(git(repo, "show", f"origin/{main}:{name}") or "")
        if m:
            return m.group(1)
    return None


def published(repo, main):
    """The paths origin/<main>'s .helloworld/allowlist.txt sends to the public twin (one per line)."""
    text = git(repo, "show", f"origin/{main}:.helloworld/allowlist.txt") or ""
    return {l.strip() for l in text.splitlines() if l.strip() and not l.lstrip().startswith("#")}


def held_slice(repo, wt, main, mode, cfg=None):
    """Why a dnd or yolo landing stops at a ready PR, or None when it may merge (the ladder, S3b).
    Every mode: the launcher's own gates (GATES, in the overlay) and a path in the base's
    `autofinish_exclude` or `in_place` — a branch never lands the gate it is judged by, nor what every
    session's hooks run; yolo's "all the way" stops there (the landing review, 2026-10-22; bin/pr
    refuses it end to end anyway). dnd adds the slice the owner kept for themself: a repo whose `physical:`
    is not none, a path the public twin publishes, a data file the branch deletes, money in the prose
    it adds, and an answer taken under yolo (no holds applied).
    Any other mode holds everything: `ask` by its rule, `default` because a taken answer never lands
    there (yolo-veto D1). What it cannot read is a hold, never a pass."""
    if mode not in LAND_MODES:
        return "mode ask — nothing lands by itself" if mode == "ask" else \
            f"mode {mode or 'default'} — a taken answer never lands there (yolo-veto D1)"
    tip = git(wt, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
    base = git(wt, "merge-base", f"origin/{main}", tip) if tip else None
    if not base:
        return f"no merge-base with origin/{main}"
    status, _, _, added, unparsed = diff_facts(wt, base, tip)
    if Path(repo).name == LAUNCHER:
        g = next((p for p in sorted(status) if p.startswith(GATES)), None)
        if g:
            return f"the launcher's own gate ({g})"
    _, records, problem, exclude = cfg or repo_config(repo, main)
    # a list that will not read fails closed; a repo with no workflow config at all has no gates to name
    if problem and not problem.startswith(("no .claude/workflow.json", "unknown autofinish value")):
        return f"the repo's config ({problem})"
    g = next((p for p in sorted(status) if exclude and p.startswith(tuple(exclude))), None)
    if g:
        return f"a gated path ({g}: autofinish_exclude or in_place)"
    if mode == "yolo":
        return None
    phys = physical_line(repo, main)
    if not phys or not phys.lower().startswith("none"):
        return f"a physical repo (physical: {phys or 'no line in the contract'})"
    pub = published(repo, main)
    g = next((p for p in sorted(status) if p in pub), None)
    if g:
        return f"a public publish ({g} goes to the public twin)"
    g = next((p for p in sorted(status) if status[p] == "D" and (p.endswith(DATA_SUFFIXES) or under(p, records))), None)
    if g:
        return f"a deletion of data ({g})"
    if unparsed:
        return f"money unread ({unparsed[0]}: unparsed diff)"
    for p in sorted(added):
        for line in added[p]:
            if YOLO_TAKEN in line:
                return f"an answer taken under yolo, the ship now dnd ({p})"
            m = MONEY_RE.search(MODEL_COST_RE.sub(" ", CODE_SPAN.sub(" ", line)))
            if m:
                return f"money in the diff ({p}: {m.group(0).strip()})"
    return None


BRIEFS = "autofinish-briefs"   # <common git dir>/claude/<it>/<branch, quoted>.json: the yes at the brief (S7)
BRANCH_RE = re.compile(r"^claude/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}-(?P<topic>[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)$")


def brief_path(repo, branch):
    """Where the yes at the brief for `branch` waits: the repo's common git dir, so a worktree made
    later finds it, and a same-named branch in a sibling repo never does (the branch-name trap)."""
    cdir = git(repo, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
    return Path(cdir) / "claude" / BRIEFS / (urllib.parse.quote(branch, safe="") + ".json") if cdir else None


def brief_yes(repo, branch):
    """The owner's yes given at the brief for `branch` (D2-C), or None: the override's shape and `by`,
    with `branch` naming this one — a stray or copied file lifts nothing."""
    p = brief_path(repo, branch) if branch else None
    try:
        m = json.loads(p.read_text()) if p else None
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    return m if isinstance(m, dict) and m.get("by") == common.OVERRIDE_BY and m.get("branch") == branch else None


def brief_held(repo, wt, main, cfg=None):
    """Why a `--by brief` landing stops at a ready PR, or None when it may merge. The yes was given
    before the diff existed (the trap on a yes older than the diff), so it lands class 1 and class 3
    and never: the launcher's own gates, the repo's `in_place`, its `.claude/workflow.json` (the list
    a yes to an unborn branch must not reshape), `.github/workflows/`, a binary, or a path under the
    base's `autofinish_leaks` — class 2, a leak with no undo. A base with no readable `autofinish_leaks`
    holds every listed path, as a sweep would, and a diff that will not read holds too: fails closed."""
    tip = git(wt, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
    base = git(wt, "merge-base", f"origin/{main}", tip) if tip else None
    if not base:
        return f"no merge-base with origin/{main}"
    status, _, binary, _, unparsed = diff_facts(wt, base, tip)
    if unparsed and not binary:  # numstat failed: a binary cannot be told from text, so hold (the re-review)
        return "the branch's diff could not be read in full"
    if not status:
        # an empty diff here is git failing or timing out as often as a branch with nothing in it: what
        # it cannot read is a hold, never a merge (landing review, finding 7)
        return "the branch's diff could not be read (or is empty)"
    if binary:
        return f"a binary ({binary[0]}): no landing carries one unread"
    paths = sorted(status)
    if Path(repo).name == LAUNCHER:
        g = next((p for p in paths if p.startswith(GATES)), None)
        if g:
            return f"the launcher's own gate ({g})"
    _, _, problem, exclude = cfg or repo_config(repo, main)
    if problem:
        return f"the repo's config ({problem})"
    try:
        raw = json.loads(git(repo, "show", f"origin/{main}:.claude/workflow.json") or "{}")
    except ValueError:
        raw = {}
    leaks = raw.get("autofinish_leaks") if isinstance(raw, dict) else None
    inplace = raw.get("in_place") if isinstance(raw, dict) and isinstance(raw.get("in_place"), list) else []
    if not isinstance(leaks, list):
        held = tuple(exclude)
        why = "no autofinish_leaks on the base: every listed path holds"
    else:
        # `.github/workflows/` beside the list: check.yml and automerge.yml run from the PR's own copy, so a
        # branch could loosen the check or the hold brake it is judged by (finding 4)
        held = tuple(p for p in leaks + inplace + [".claude/workflow.json", ".github/workflows/"] if isinstance(p, str) and p)
        why = "class 2, in_place, the list or the workflows: a yes at the brief never lands it"
    g = next((p for p in paths if held and p.startswith(held)), None)
    return f"a held path ({g}; {why})" if g else None


def mode_asks(wt, asks, status, fetch):
    """(skip, taken) — rule M's asks gate: skip is None when every asks line is in a D-list doc the
    branch adds and every such doc is settled — `answered`, `stood`, or `taken` (keel yolo's picks,
    which this mode lands); taken lists the `taken` docs, which only rule M may start."""
    docs = sorted({p for p, _, dl in asks if dl and status.get(p) == "A" and p.endswith(".md")})
    stray = next((h for h in asks if not h[2] or h[0] not in docs), None)
    if stray:
        return f"skip: asks ({stray[0]}: {stray[1]})", []
    taken = []
    for doc in docs:
        s = memo_status(wt, doc, fetch)
        if s is None:
            return f"skip: memo status unavailable ({doc})", []
        if s.get("verdict") not in ("answered", "stood", "taken"):
            return f"skip: asks ({doc}: memo {s.get('verdict')} — lands once nothing is open)", []
        if s.get("verdict") == "taken":
            taken.append(doc)
    return None, taken


DATED_LOG = re.compile(r"^autofinish-(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})\.log$")  # not autofinish-launchd.log


def first_arm():
    """The launch door's epoch: the earliest of `first-armed` (arm and disarm keep it, so a
    re-arm does not reset the count to 0/5 — widen review) and the live `armed`'s ts; None when
    this Mac was never armed. Deleting `first-armed` is the deliberate reset."""
    ts = [t for t in (first_armed_ts(), iso_ts((armed() or {}).get("ts"))) if t is not None]
    return min(ts) if ts else None


def first_armed_ts():
    try:
        return iso_ts(json.loads((STATE / "first-armed").read_text()).get("ts"))
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None


def launches_since_arm():
    """(n, armed) — how many runs the sweep started (`by=sweep`) launched at or after this Mac's
    first arm, from the dated logs; (0, False) while disarmed. The launch door: E, and B in a
    repo that was not "small" or "all" at the first arm, open at E_LAUNCHES.
    Not <git dir>/claude/autofinished: it records the owner's typed /finish hand-offs and /autofinish
    runs too, about three a day, which would open the door before autofinish chose five (review)."""
    since = first_arm() if armed() else None
    if since is None:
        return 0, False
    first = (datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(since) - datetime.timedelta(days=1)).strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
    seen = set()
    for path in sorted(LOG_DIR.glob("autofinish-*.log")):
        day = DATED_LOG.match(path.name)
        if not day or day.group(1) < first:
            continue
        try:
            text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
        except OSError:
            continue
        for ln in text.splitlines():
            m = common.RUN_LAUNCHED.match(ln) if " landed " in ln or " launched " in ln else None  # most lines are dry verdicts
            # rule L's launches are the deploy's, not the sweep's judgement: they never open the door
            t = iso_ts(m.group(1)) if m and m.group(5) == "sweep" and " rule=L " not in ln else None
            if t is not None and t >= since:
                seen.add((m.group(2), m.group(3), m.group(4)))
                if len(seen) >= E_LAUNCHES:  # the door is open: no need to read months of logs (review)
                    return len(seen), True
    return len(seen), True


_was = {}


def mode_at_first_arm(repo, main):
    """The repo's autofinish value on origin/<main> at the first arm: its last first-parent
    commit at or before that moment. None when this Mac was never armed, or origin/<main> has no
    commit that old. The door spares B only where this is "small" or "all": B ran there before the
    arm, so "all" opens nothing new for it (the owner, 2026-10-08, the widen doc's item 2); a repo
    that turned "small" after the arm (shop/books) waits like any other, and so does None (review
    at /finish). Read by committer date; a flip rebased in after the arm reads as the mode at the
    arm. An overlay repo reads "off" (repo_config's `at`). verdict() does not call this before the first arm: there a "small" repo keeps its B and an
    "all" one waits."""
    key = str(repo)
    if key in _was:
        return _was[key]
    with _CACHE:
        if key not in _was:
            since, mode = first_arm(), None
            sha = since is not None and git(repo, "rev-list", "-1", "--first-parent",
                                            f"--before={int(since)}", f"origin/{main}")
            if sha:
                mode = repo_config(repo, main, sha)[0]
            _was[key] = mode
        return _was[key]


_launches = None


def launches():
    global _launches
    if _launches is not None:
        return _launches
    with _CACHE:
        if _launches is None:
            _launches = launches_since_arm()
        return _launches


def under(path, prefixes):
    """knowledge-commit's prefix syntax: `x/` is a folder, anything else one file."""
    return any(path.startswith(p) if p.endswith("/") else path == p for p in prefixes)


override = common.autofinish_override  # rule C's marker: one reader, shared with checkpoint.py


def running(pid, proc_start):
    """bin/pickup's test: alive, and the same process (procStart is `ps -o lstart` in UTC)."""
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
    except PermissionError:
        pass
    except OSError:
        return False
    if not proc_start:
        return True
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "lstart=", "-p", str(pid)], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=5, env={**os.environ, "TZ": "UTC", "LC_ALL": "C"})
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return True  # can't tell: count it alive, the safe side for a veto
    return " ".join(r.stdout.split()) == " ".join(proc_start.split())


_live = None


def live_sessions():
    global _live
    if _live is not None:  # published whole (below), so a reader never takes it half-filled
        return _live
    with _CACHE:  # a half-filled list read by another judging thread would miss a busy session
        if _live is None:
            live = []
            for f in REGISTRY.glob("*.json"):
                try:
                    e = json.loads(f.read_text())
                except (OSError, ValueError):
                    continue
                if isinstance(e, dict) and isinstance(e.get("pid"), int) and e.get("sessionId") \
                        and running(e["pid"], e.get("procStart")):
                    live.append(e)
            _live = live
        return _live


def inside(path, root):
    try:
        p, r = Path(path).resolve(), Path(root).resolve()
    except OSError:
        return False
    return p == r or r in p.parents


def sessions_on(worktree, ids):
    """Live registry entries on this worktree: by id (the registry's cwd stays where a session
    started, before EnterWorktree), or by cwd."""
    return [e for e in live_sessions()
            if e["sessionId"] in ids or (e.get("cwd") and inside(e["cwd"], worktree))]


def ancestors():
    """The pids above this process: a session that runs `autofinish` (a hook, /autofinish's
    Bash) is one of them, and it is busy only because it is running us."""
    pids, pid = set(), os.getpid()
    for _ in range(40):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "ppid=", "-p", str(pid)], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=5)
            pid = int(r.stdout.strip() or 0)
        except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            break
        if pid <= 1:
            break
        pids.add(pid)
    return pids


def own_sessions(session=None):
    """(ids, pids) never `busy` for this evaluation: the caller's session at its turn's end
    (--session, a full id or its first 8) and every live session among our ancestors."""
    return ({session} if session else set()), ancestors()


def is_own(entry, own):
    if not own:
        return False
    ids, pids = own
    sid = entry.get("sessionId") or ""
    return entry.get("pid") in pids or any(i and (sid == i or sid.startswith(i)) for i in ids)


def queue_session(state):
    try:
        q = json.loads((state / "prompts.json").read_text())
        return next((e.get("session") for e in reversed(q) if isinstance(e, dict) and e.get("session")), None)
    except (OSError, ValueError, TypeError):
        return None


def transcript(sid):
    """The newest transcript for a full id or an 8-hex prefix, or None."""
    if not sid:
        return None
    hits = [p for p in PROJECTS.glob(f"*/{sid}*.jsonl") if p.is_file()]
    return max(hits, key=lambda p: p.stat().st_mtime) if hits else None


def last_reply(path):
    """The assistant text since the last human prompt (bin/paste-paths' last_reply), read from
    the file's tail. None when the file can't be read or holds no reply."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            fh.seek(0, 2)
            fh.seek(max(0, fh.tell() - TAIL_BYTES))
            lines = fh.read().decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None
    texts = []
    for raw in reversed(lines):
        try:
            e = json.loads(raw)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if not isinstance(e, dict) or e.get("isSidechain"):
            continue
        content = (e.get("message") or {}).get("content")
        if e.get("type") == "user":
            if isinstance(content, str) or not any(
                    isinstance(c, dict) and c.get("type") == "tool_result" for c in content or []):
                break
            continue
        if e.get("type") == "assistant" and isinstance(content, list):
            texts.extend(c.get("text", "") for c in reversed(content)
                         if isinstance(c, dict) and c.get("type") == "text")
    return "\n".join(reversed(texts)) if texts else None


def reply_veto(text, live=True):
    """Why this reply leaves the next move to the owner, or None. `live`: a session is on the branch —
    a WAITING FOR SOMETHING reply, or the hand-over line, vetoes only then: with its session gone, nothing
    wakes it, and a hand-over dispatch declined (disarmed, the week) is the sweep's to take once it may."""
    kind, _, _ = common.ending(text)
    if kind == "waiting":
        return f"waiting for {OWNER} (last reply ends in the WAITING line)"
    if kind == "relayed":  # session-asks R4: an ask is out with its skipper; the answer comes back here
        return "waiting on its skipper (last reply ends in the RELAYED line)"
    if kind == "pending" and live:  # mid-work: its reviewers, check or poll wake it, never launched under it
        return "waiting on its own work (last reply ends in the WAITING FOR SOMETHING line)"
    if kind == "handed" and live:  # dispatch's launch takes it as the run ends: never a second start under it
        return "handed to dispatch's launch (last reply ends in the hand-over line)"
    for line in reversed(text.splitlines()):
        line = common.bare(line.strip())
        if not line or LINK_ONLY.match(line):
            continue
        return "last reply asks (ends in ?)" if line.endswith("?") else None
    return None


def mtime(p):
    try:
        return p.stat().st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return None


ACCOUNT = os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_ACCOUNT") or str(common.tool("account") or BIN / "account")
PICK_TIMEOUT = 90  # a fresh pick probes each row it reaches (~4 s, two tries of 30 s at worst)
# The login variables bin/account must not inherit: a token outranks the dir it probes (account
# run strips it too), and the runner gets neither from start_runner, so the gate and the run pick alike.
LOGIN_ENV = ("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", "CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN")


_TC = None


def tier_check():
    """hooks/tier_check.py, loaded once. It reads CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR at import: hidden here, so a
    a work-login caller (a session-end child of a work-login run) reads the default login, as named."""
    global _TC
    if _TC is not None:
        return _TC
    with _CACHE:
        # The pop below must not overlap a judging thread: one copying os.environ (dict(os.environ,
        # …) in branches.probably, dispatch-rc) meets a KeyError mid-copy. dry_run() loads this
        # before its pool starts, so the window is single-threaded (review, 2026-10-18).
        if _TC is None:
            if os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_NOW"):
                os.environ.setdefault("CLAUDE_TIER_NOW", os.environ["AUTOFINISH_NOW"])
            from importlib.machinery import SourceFileLoader
            cfg = os.environ.pop("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", None)
            try:
                _TC = SourceFileLoader("tier_check", str(common.tool("tier_check.py") or BIN / "tier_check.py")).load_module()
            finally:
                if cfg is not None:
                    os.environ["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = cfg
        return _TC


def tightness(pct, sampled, resets, who=""):
    """(tight, detail) by review-gate's threshold; (False, None) for last week's figure or no reading
    — review-gate's rule: no reading is the normal budget."""
    try:
        tc = tier_check()
        pct = float(pct)
        if not resets:
            return pct >= tc.LOW, f"{who}{pct:g}% used"
        resets = float(resets)
        while resets < tc.now():  # a cached reset in the past: the week rolled over since (tier_check's weekly)
            resets += tc.WEEK
        if sampled and float(sampled) < resets - tc.WEEK:  # sampled before this week began: last week's figure
            return False, None
        pace = tc.week_pace(resets)
        return (pct > pace + tc.AHEAD or pct >= tc.LOW), f"{who}{pct:g}% used, pace {pace:g}%"
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return False, None


def default_week(who=""):
    """tier_check's reading of the default login (~/.claude.json, CLAUDE_TIER_CLAUDE_JSON in tests)."""
    try:
        tc = tier_check()
        w = tc.weekly(tc.load(tc.CLAUDE_JSON))
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return False, None
    return tightness(w[0], w[1] / 1000, w[2], who) if w else (False, None)


def account(*args, timeout=PICK_TIMEOUT):
    """(exit, parsed JSON or None) of bin/account; exit None when it could not run."""
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k not in LOGIN_ENV}
    try:
        r = common.run_group([ACCOUNT, *args], timeout, env=env)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None, None
    try:
        return r.returncode, json.loads(r.stdout)
    except ValueError:
        return r.returncode, None


def week(repo, fresh=False):
    """→ (verdict, detail): ("tight", …), ("none", reasons) when no account may run, or
    (None, detail) — the row `account pick --repo` takes for this repo (header, week). `fresh`
    probes (the judging a start follows); every other judging reads the cached readings only,
    so a dry run, the Stop hook's check and a disarmed sweep spawn no probe."""
    rc, d = account("pick", "--repo", str(repo), "--json", *([] if fresh else ["--cached"]))
    if rc == 3 and isinstance(d, dict):
        return "none", "; ".join(f"{l}: {w}" for l, w in d.get("skipped") or []) or "the registry has no row"
    if rc != 0 or not isinstance(d, dict) or not d.get("account"):  # exit 4 (no registry) included
        tight, detail = default_week()
        return ("tight" if tight else None), detail
    label = d["account"]
    if fresh:
        _picked[str(repo)] = (d.get("env") or {}).get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", "")
    if not d.get("primary"):
        # a secondary's week is its own registry ceiling (week_max, its reserve), which the pick
        # just applied: review-gate's pace rule guards the owner's own week, not a seat meant to be
        # spent — on the work login's last day the pick allows 100 % (review, 2026-10-08)
        return None, f"{label}: {d.get('reason') or 'picked'}"
    rc, rows = account("limits", label, "--cached", "--json", timeout=30)
    row = rows[0] if rc == 0 and isinstance(rows, list) and rows and isinstance(rows[0], dict) else {}
    lim = row.get("limits") or {}
    if lim.get("seven_day") is None:
        if not d.get("env"):  # the default login: tier_check's reading, as before the pool
            tight, detail = default_week(f"{label} ")
            return ("tight" if tight else None), detail
        return None, f"{label}: no week reading"
    tight, detail = tightness(lim["seven_day"], row.get("sampled"), lim.get("seven_day_resets"), f"{label} ")
    return ("tight" if tight else None), detail


_week = {}
_picked = {}  # repo → the CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR of its last fresh pick ("" the default login)


def week_tight(repo, fresh=False):
    """week(repo), once per repo and freshness per process: the sweep judges many branches of one
    repo. attempt() clears it before the judging a start follows. An odd answer is no account,
    never a crashed sweep (review, 2026-10-08)."""
    key = (str(repo), bool(fresh))
    hit = _week.get(key)  # one read: attempt() clears _week, though only after the pool has joined
    if hit is not None:
        return hit
    with _CACHE:  # one bin/account per repo, however many judging threads reach it at once
        if key not in _week:
            try:
                _week[key] = week(repo, fresh)
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
                _week[key] = ("none", f"the pick could not be read ({type(e).__name__})")
        return _week[key]


def memo_status(worktree, doc, fetch=False):
    """bin/keel's verdict for one doc as a dict, or None when memo can't say. The contract
    (E10, built in parallel): JSON with `verdict` in stands-at|due|stood|part-stood|voided|too-big|
    physical|no-contract|taken|answered, `deadline` (ISO or null) and, since S3, `unstandable`. `fetch` only for the judging a
    start follows: a dry run writes no remote-tracking ref, and is no slower for GitHub."""
    cmd = [MEMO, "status", "--json"] + ([] if fetch else ["--no-fetch"]) + [doc]
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(cmd, cwd=str(worktree), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=MEMO_TIMEOUT)
        if r.returncode:
            return None
        s = json.loads(r.stdout)
    except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    if isinstance(s, list):
        s = s[0] if s else None
    return s if isinstance(s, dict) and s.get("verdict") else None


_remote = {}


def origin_tip(repo, branch):
    """origin's tip of `branch` from `git ls-remote` (read-only, unlike a fetch): sha, "" when
    origin has no such branch, None when origin could not be asked."""
    key = str(repo)
    if key not in _remote:
        with _CACHE:  # one ls-remote per repo, however many judging threads reach it: origin is GitHub
            if key not in _remote:
                text = git(repo, "ls-remote", "origin", "refs/heads/claude/*", timeout=30)
                _remote[key] = None if text is None else dict(
                    (l.split("\t")[1][len("refs/heads/"):], l.split("\t")[0]) for l in text.splitlines() if "\t" in l)
            tips = _remote[key]
    else:
        tips = _remote.get(key)  # one read: attempt() pops _remote, though only after the pool has joined
    return None if tips is None else tips.get(branch, "")


def github_state(repo, branch, since):
    """{"pr": {number, title, draft} or None, "activity": comments and reviews on it after
    `since`}, or None when GitHub could not be asked. The only GitHub reader here, so the tests
    stub exactly this (AUTOFINISH_GITHUB)."""
    fixture = os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_GITHUB")
    if fixture:
        try:
            data = json.loads(Path(fixture).read_text())
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return None
        return data.get(branch) or {"pr": None, "activity": 0}
    slug = common.repo_slug(git(repo, "remote", "get-url", "origin") or "")
    tok = common.token()
    if not slug or not tok:
        return None
    head = urllib.parse.quote(f"{slug.split('/')[0]}:{branch}", safe=":")
    st, prs = common.api("GET", f"/repos/{slug}/pulls?state=open&per_page=5&head={head}", tok=tok)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(prs, list):
        return None
    if not prs:
        return {"pr": None, "activity": 0}
    pr = prs[0]
    iso = datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(since, datetime.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
    n = 0
    for path in (f"/repos/{slug}/issues/{pr['number']}/comments?since={iso}&per_page=50",
                 f"/repos/{slug}/pulls/{pr['number']}/comments?since={iso}&per_page=50"):
        st, items = common.api("GET", path, tok=tok)
        if st != 200 or not isinstance(items, list):
            return None
        n += sum(1 for c in items if not str((c or {}).get("body") or "").startswith(DIED_HEAD))
    st, reviews = common.api("GET", f"/repos/{slug}/pulls/{pr['number']}/reviews?per_page=50", tok=tok)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(reviews, list):
        return None
    n += sum(1 for r in reviews if (r.get("submitted_at") or "") > iso)
    return {"pr": {"number": pr["number"], "title": pr.get("title") or "", "draft": pr.get("draft")},
            "activity": n}


# ---- the diff -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

def raw(repo, *args, timeout=30):
    """git's stdout unstripped (a -z listing ends in NUL, a diff's lines keep their spaces), or None."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    return r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else None


def added_lines(wt, base, tip, path):
    """The lines one path's diff adds, or None when the diff can't be read line by line. Its own
    diff, so no header line can be taken for a path: the first review found `+++ b/<path>`
    parsing lost a name with a space (git appends a TAB) and let an added `++ b/x` line re-target
    every line after it. Header lines are skipped up to the first `@@`; after it every line is a
    hunk header, `+`, `-`, ` ` or `\\ No newline`, or the diff is not what this reads."""
    out = raw(wt, "-c", "core.quotePath=false", "diff", "--no-renames", "--no-color", "--no-ext-diff",
              "--no-textconv", "-U0", base, tip, "--", path, timeout=60)
    if out is None:
        return None
    lines, hunks = [], False
    for line in out.split("\n")[:-1] if out.endswith("\n") else out.split("\n"):
        if line.startswith("@@"):
            hunks = True
        elif not hunks:
            continue
        elif line.startswith("+"):
            lines.append(line[1:])
        elif not line.startswith(("-", " ", "\\")):
            return None
    return lines


def diff_facts(wt, base, tip):
    """Paths with their status, inserted lines, binaries, every added line per text path, and the
    text paths whose added lines could not be read (each an asks veto: never a silent pass)."""
    status = {}
    fields = (raw(wt, "diff", "--no-renames", "--name-status", "-z", base, tip) or "").split("\0")
    for st, path in zip(fields[0::2], fields[1::2]):
        if path:
            status[path] = st[:1]
    inserted, binary, counts = 0, [], {}
    numstat = raw(wt, "diff", "--no-renames", "--numstat", "-z", base, tip)
    if numstat is None and status:
        # git failed or timed out (likelier with JUDGE_THREADS at once): no counts means no binary
        # veto and no size, so every path is unread rather than a silent pass (review, 2026-10-18)
        return status, 0, [], {}, sorted(status)
    for rec in (numstat or "").split("\0"):
        parts = rec.split("\t", 2)
        if len(parts) == 3:
            if parts[0] == "-":
                binary.append(parts[2])
            elif parts[0].isdigit():
                inserted += int(parts[0])
                counts[parts[2]] = int(parts[0])
    added, unparsed = {}, []
    for path in sorted(status):
        if status[path] == "D" or not path.endswith(TEXT_SUFFIXES) or path in binary:
            continue
        lines = added_lines(wt, base, tip, path)
        # numstat's count is the cross-check: a line the hunks did not yield is a line unread
        if lines is None or len(lines) != counts.get(path, len(lines)):
            unparsed.append(path)
        elif lines:
            added[path] = lines
    return status, inserted, binary, added, unparsed


def asks_in(path, lines, st="M"):
    """[(path, marker, is_dlist_marker)] for every line that asks the owner something. Lines are read
    in text files only: in code the markers are fixtures and regexes (keel's tests/keel.sh in regatta,
    bin/dispatch on the first real run, 2026-10-05), and a code file never passes rule A anyway."""
    hits = []
    if path.startswith(HANDOFF) and st != "D":
        hits.append((path, "a seed under docs/handoff/", False))
    if not path.endswith(TEXT_SUFFIXES):
        return hits
    for raw in lines:
        if branches.asks_answer(raw):
            hits.append((path, "➤ Answer:", True))
            continue
        line = CODE_SPAN.sub("", raw)
        if D_ITEM.search(line):
            hits.append((path, "**D<n> —**", True))
        elif DECIDE.search(line):
            hits.append((path, "**Decide:**", False))
        elif CHECKBOX.match(line):
            hits.append((path, "- [ ]", False))
        elif HANDOFF in line:
            hits.append((path, "a docs/handoff/ line", False))
    return hits


# ---- one worktree -------------------------------------------------------------------------

def iso_ts(iso):
    try:
        return datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(str(iso).replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except ValueError:
        return None


def spoken_since(state, rec, ov):
    """True when anyone spoke to the branch after its last run ended: a prompt queued by a session
    other than the run's, a turn's end (last-reply: the runner's own last turn ends at least the
    watcher's 10 idle minutes before `ended`), or an override marker set since. prompts.json
    alone would not do: a checkpoint commit takes its session's prompts out of the queue."""
    ended = iso_ts(rec.get("ended"))
    if ended is None:
        return True  # an end it can't read is no stop to wait on
    try:
        q = json.loads((state / "prompts.json").read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        q = []
    for e in q if isinstance(q, list) else []:
        if isinstance(e, dict) and e.get("session") != rec.get("session") \
                and isinstance(e.get("ts"), (int, float)) and e["ts"] > ended:
            return True
    if (mtime(state / "last-reply") or 0) > ended:
        return True
    if ov:
        ts = ov.get("ts")
        return (ts if isinstance(ts, (int, float)) else mtime(state / common.AUTOFINISH_OVERRIDE) or 0) > ended
    return False


_runner = None


def runner():
    """bin/autofinish-run as a module: dead() and bury() are the runner's, beside the `finally`
    whose writes they stand in for."""
    global _runner
    if _runner is not None:
        return _runner
    with _CACHE:  # published once exec_module has filled it: no judging thread takes it half-made
        if _runner is None:
            import importlib.machinery
            import importlib.util
            loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("autofinish_run", str(BIN / "autofinish-run"))
            mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader))
            loader.exec_module(mod)
            _runner = mod
        return _runner


def dead_run(state, run):
    """Why the worktree's unended run is dead, or None (bin/autofinish-run's dead()); `state` is
    its <git dir>/claude, where the waiter's pid is."""
    if not run or run.get("ended"):
        return None
    return runner().dead(None, run, now=now(), state_dir=str(state))


def died_twice(wt, branch):
    """The date of the second death when the branch's last two ended runs both died on one tip (the
    retry of a death dying too, not two unrelated power cuts), else None."""
    cdir = git(wt, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
    ends = [e for e in common.autofinished(cdir) if e["branch"] == branch] if cdir else []
    if len(ends) < 2 or not all(e["outcome"] == DIED for e in ends[-2:]):
        return None
    tips = [(e["detail"] or "").split(" ", 1)[0] for e in ends[-2:]]
    return ends[-1]["ts"][:10] if tips[0] and tips[0] == tips[1] else None


def own_push(wt, run, remote):
    """True when origin's tip is the dead runner's own push: its recorded tip, or descendants of it
    every one of which carries the session's `Claude-Session:` trailer (checkpoint.py's, 8 hex).
    Origin's commit not here (no fetch while judging) is no proof: moved, as before."""
    tip0, sid = run.get("tip"), str(run.get("session") or "")[:8]
    if not tip0 or not sid:
        return False
    if remote == tip0:
        return True
    if git(wt, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", tip0, remote) is None:
        return False
    out = git(wt, "log", "--format=%H%x1f%(trailers:key=Claude-Session,valueonly,separator=%x2C)",
              f"{tip0}..{remote}")
    rows = [l.split("\x1f") for l in (out or "").splitlines() if l]
    return bool(rows) and all(len(r) == 2 and sid in r[1].split(",") for r in rows)


def bury_dead(repos):
    """The sweep's first pass: each worktree whose run died is recorded so (bin/autofinish-run's
    bury: state, lock, PR comment, record, log), and the line printed for launchd's log."""
    for name, repo in repos:
        for wt, branch, gitdir in branches.worktrees(repo):
            if not branch or not branch.startswith("claude/") or gitdir is None or not Path(wt).is_dir():
                continue  # a prunable worktree: its Tree() would answer with empty paths
            run = common.autofinish_run(Path(gitdir) / "claude")
            if not run or run.get("ended"):
                continue
            try:
                why = dead_run(Path(gitdir) / "claude", run)
                if why and runner().bury(runner().Tree(str(wt)), why):
                    print(f"died {name} {branch} — {why}", flush=True)
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — one odd run must not stop the sweep
                print(f"autofinish: {name} {branch}: could not judge its run ({type(e).__name__}: {str(e)[:80]})",
                      flush=True)


# the docs a `start (D)` verdict found `due`, per worktree: what attempt() applies before the start
_due = {}


def deployed_on(wt, base, tip):
    """(live, before): the first `Deployed:` value in base..tip that says something went live
    (common.deployed), from a commit made at or after common.L_SINCE — rule L — and the first from
    an older one. Every commit, not only the tip: a later turn that deployed nothing writes no
    line, and the live system still runs the branch (review at /finish, 2026-10-14)."""
    live = before = None
    for rec in (git(wt, "log", "--format=%ct%x1f%B%x1e", f"{base}..{tip}") or "").split("\x1e"):
        ct, sep, body = rec.strip().partition("\x1f")
        value = common.deployed(body) if sep else None
        if not value:
            continue
        if float(ct or 0) >= common.L_SINCE:
            live = live or value
        else:
            before = before or value
    return live, before


def verdict(repo, wt, branch, gitdir, main, cfg, own=None, reply=None, fetch=False):
    """`start (X)` / `wait: idle N min` / `skip: <veto>` for one claude/* worktree. `own`: the
    sessions never busy here (own_sessions); `reply`: the last reply's text in place of the
    transcript's (the Stop hook's, read before the transcript may hold it); `fetch`: memo status
    fetches first (the judging a start follows)."""
    mode, records, problem, exclude = cfg
    if not wt.is_dir():
        return "skip: worktree missing"
    state = gitdir / "claude"
    ov = override(state) or brief_yes(repo, branch)   # the worktree's marker, else the yes at the brief (S7)
    if mode == "off" and not ov:
        return "skip: off" + (f" ({problem})" if problem else "")

    m = common.moved(state)
    if m:
        return f"skip: moved ({common.moved_line(m)})"
    run = common.autofinish_run(state)
    why = dead_run(state, run)
    if why:
        return f"skip: died, not recorded yet ({why}; session {str(run.get('session'))[:8]}) — an armed sweep records it"
    owner = (common.read_owner(state) or {}).get("session")
    tip = git(wt, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
    message = git(wt, "log", "-1", "--format=%B", "HEAD") or ""
    trailer = re.search(r"^Claude-Session:\s*([0-9a-f]{8})\s*$", message, re.M)
    ids = {s for s in (owner, queue_session(state)) if s}
    on = sessions_on(wt, ids)
    # anything but idle: `waiting` is a dialog or a permission prompt in front of the owner, `shell` a
    # command running — the CLI's registry has more states than busy and idle (review, 2026-10-05)
    busy = [e for e in on if e.get("status") != "idle" and not is_own(e, own)]
    if busy:
        st = busy[0].get("status")
        return f"skip: busy (session {busy[0]['sessionId'][:8]}" + ("" if st == "busy" else f", {st or 'no status'}") + ")"
    fin = mtime(state / "finishing")
    if fin and now() - fin < FINISHING_TTL:
        return "skip: finishing (a /finish is under way)"
    gb = next((x for x in GIT_BUSY if (gitdir / x).exists()), None)
    if gb:
        return f"skip: git busy ({gb})"
    dirty = branches.uncommitted(wt)
    if dirty is None or dirty:
        return f"skip: uncommitted ({dirty if dirty else '?'} files)"
    if not tip:
        return "skip: no commit"

    tgt = f"origin/{main}"
    # a worktree nothing was committed in yet is on main too, but it is not "launched"
    if branches.is_ancestor(repo, tip, tgt) and branches.committed_nothing(repo, branch, tip):
        return "skip: nothing committed"
    b = branches.Branch(branch)
    b.local = tip
    proof = branches.prove(repo, b, tgt)
    if proof:
        return f"skip: launched ({proof})"
    hint = branches.probably(repo, b, tgt)
    if hint:
        return f"skip: pickup review ({hint})"
    if run and run.get("ended") and run.get("outcome") in ("stopped", "never-started") \
            and run.get("gate") not in REFUSED_GATES and not spoken_since(state, run, ov):
        return f"skip: stopped at {run.get('gate') or '?'} ({run.get('ended')}) — waits for a prompt"
    retry = bool(run and run.get("ended") and run.get("outcome") == DIED)
    if retry:
        twice = died_twice(wt, branch)
        if twice and not spoken_since(state, run, ov):
            return f"skip: died twice ({twice}) — waits for a prompt"
        tip0 = run.get("tip")
        if tip0 and tip and tip != tip0 and git(wt, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", tip0, tip) is None \
                and not spoken_since(state, run, ov):
            return (f"skip: died mid-curation (local rewritten since {tip0[:7]}; {branch}-raw may hold it)"
                    " — waits for a prompt")
    base = git(wt, "merge-base", tgt, tip)
    if not base:
        return f"skip: no merge-base with {tgt}"
    live = None  # rule L's deploy, read below (not for rule D)
    status, inserted, binary, added, unparsed = diff_facts(wt, base, tip)
    if not status:
        return "skip: nothing to launch"
    if any(p.startswith("docs/handoff/") for p in status):
        why = open_steps(wt, base)
        if why:
            return f"skip: open step ({why})"
    if unparsed:
        return f"skip: asks ({unparsed[0]}: unparsed diff)"

    # asks, and rule D — the only start a branch with asks lines can get, bar rule M
    asks = [h for p in sorted(status) for h in asks_in(p, added.get(p, []), status[p])]
    rule, docs, beside = None, [], None
    _due.pop(str(wt), None)
    # the landing ladder (S3b): a dnd or yolo branch is judged by rule M, its taken docs no veto
    lmode = None if ov else landing_mode(branch, repo)[0]
    taken = []
    if lmode in LAND_MODES:
        skip, taken = mode_asks(wt, asks, status, fetch) if asks else (None, [])
        if skip:
            return skip
        asks = []
    if asks:
        docs = sorted({p for p, _, dl in asks if dl and status.get(p) == "A" and p.endswith(".md")})
        if not docs or any(not dl or p not in docs for p, _, dl in asks):
            p, marker, _ = next(h for h in asks if not h[2] or h[0] not in docs)
            return f"skip: asks ({p}: {marker})"
        # shape first: a doc with code beside it is a build, never D, whatever memo says — and
        # that reason outlives memo's (first real run: memo-form-github read "memo unavailable")
        if binary:
            return f"skip: binary ({binary[0]})"
        beside = next((p for p in sorted(status) if not under(p, records) or not p.endswith(TEXT_SUFFIXES)), None)
        if beside and mode != "all":  # item 7 named "all" only: small repos keep main's veto (review)
            return f"skip: not records ({beside})" if not under(beside, records) else f"skip: not text ({beside})"
    if beside:
        # an "all" repo: the design doc of a build the owner answered in full is no ask any
        # more, and the build is judged as code (B or E) — never stood in for the owner, never D (widen review)
        for doc in docs:
            s = memo_status(wt, doc, fetch)
            if s is None:
                return f"skip: memo status unavailable ({doc})"
            if s.get("verdict") != "answered":
                return f"skip: asks ({doc}: memo {s.get('verdict')}, beside code — launches once answered)"
        asks = []
    if asks:
        # due: the start applies it first. stood: applied already — by an earlier start whose
        # runner never ran, say — but only by this launcher's commit on this branch, not a trailer
        # anywhere memo looks. answered: the owner answered every item; /finish takes it from there.
        due = []
        for doc in docs:
            s = memo_status(wt, doc, fetch)
            if s is None:
                return f"skip: memo status unavailable ({doc})"
            v = s.get("verdict")
            if v == "part-stood":
                # entry D4-A (S3): a stand takes only Reversible: yes items with a bare pick; the rest
                # wait on the owner, so no branch launches with `no` or unmarked items open
                return f"skip: asks ({doc}: memo part-stood — {s.get('reason') or 'items still open'})"
            if v == "due" and s.get("unstandable"):
                # the stand would leave it part-stood, and /finish would then stop on the open items
                left = ", ".join(f"{i} {w}" for i, w in s["unstandable"])
                return f"skip: asks ({doc}: memo due, but a stand leaves {left} open)"
            if v == "stood" and not git(wt, "log", "-1", "--format=%h", "--fixed-strings",
                                        f"--grep={STOOD_BY}", f"{base}..{tip}", "--", doc):
                return f"skip: asks ({doc}: memo stood, but no `{STOOD_BY}` commit on the branch)"
            if v == "due" and lmode == "ask":
                # keel's stand_status says ask-mode itself; this holds where an older keel says due
                return f"skip: asks ({doc}: memo due, but mode ask — nobody answers it but {OWNER})"
            if v == "due":
                due.append(doc)
            elif v not in ("stood", "answered"):
                when = f" {s['deadline']}" if s.get("deadline") else ""
                return f"skip: asks ({doc}: memo {v}{when})"
        skip = gate_skip(repo, main, exclude, status)  # a records prefix can hold a gate
        if skip:
            return skip
        _due[str(wt)] = due
        rule = "D"
    else:
        live, before = deployed_on(wt, base, tip)
        if re.search(r"open item", message, re.I):
            return "skip: open item in the tip's message"
        if ov:
            rule = "C"
        elif lmode in LAND_MODES and (taken or not live):
            # rule M (S3b): the mode is the owner's word for this ship — any shape, size and host, no launch
            # door; gates are held at a ready PR by the runner (held_slice), never skipped here. A deployed
            # one only when it holds taken docs: rule L's start would be `--by sweep`, its taken docs read
            # as open by /finish and its held slice never applied (landing review 18); it keeps L's at-once
            # clock and the week does not pause it, as rule L's. A deployed one without taken docs is rule
            # L's, below: M would hold a physical repo's deploy at a ready PR where L launches it at once,
            # and live ahead of main is drift (skipper-modes follow-ups, item 1)
            if binary:
                return f"skip: binary ({binary[0]})"
            rule = "M"
            kind, detail = week_tight(repo)
            if kind == "none":
                return f"skip: no account ({detail})"
            if kind == "tight" and not live:
                return f"skip: week tight ({detail})"
        elif before and not live:
            # deployed before rule L existed: those branches waited for a typed /finish, and one
            # was a stale +1,011-line branch whose deploy had been put back (review at
            # /finish, 2026-10-14) — they keep that wait rather than launch unread on the first sweep
            return f"skip: deployed before rule L ({before[:60]}) — launches by /finish"
        elif live:
            # rule L: the live system already runs this branch, so main is behind it until it
            # launches — launch it at once, any shape (the owner, 2026-10-13: "If the code is deployed
            # already, it should be launched ASAP - no wait - otherwise we have a drift between the
            # code and the live state"). Until then a deploy was a skip that waited on /finish.
            if binary:
                return f"skip: binary ({binary[0]})"
            skip = gate_skip(repo, main, exclude, status)
            if skip:
                return skip
            rule = "L"
        else:
            if binary:
                return f"skip: binary ({binary[0]})"
            outside = next((p for p in sorted(status) if not under(p, records)), None)
            untext = next((p for p in sorted(status) if not p.endswith(TEXT_SUFFIXES)), None)
            if not outside and not untext:
                skip = gate_skip(repo, main, exclude, status)  # in_place and excluded paths too
                if skip:
                    return skip
                rule = "A"
            elif mode not in ("small", "all"):
                return f"skip: not records ({outside})" if outside else f"skip: not text ({untext})"
            elif inserted >= SMALL_LINES and mode == "small":
                return f"skip: too big for B ({inserted} inserted lines, needs < {SMALL_LINES})"
            else:
                rule = "B" if inserted < SMALL_LINES else "E"  # under "all", B first where it fits
                # first: it holds on any host and any count, so it is the reason worth naming
                skip = gate_skip(repo, main, exclude, status)
                if skip:
                    return skip
                if host() != LAPTOP:
                    return f"skip: {rule} is laptop only (host {host()})"
                # the door: E always; B too unless B already ran there at the first arm. Records
                # or off then, a "small" that launched after the arm, and a mode that cannot be
                # read (no history that old) all wait: the door fails closed (review at /finish).
                # Never armed, there is no arm to read: a "small" repo keeps the B it always had,
                # an "all" one waits as before
                was = None if rule == "E" else mode_at_first_arm(repo, main) if first_arm() is not None \
                    else mode if mode == "small" else None
                if rule == "E" or was not in ("small", "all"):
                    n, armed_on = launches()  # not `on`: that is the live sessions, read below
                    if n < E_LAUNCHES:
                        return f"skip: {rule} waits for {E_LAUNCHES} launches ({f'{n}/{E_LAUNCHES}' if armed_on else 'disarmed'})"
            # cached: the fresh pick is attempt()'s, after the idle clock — a session end's
            # judging would otherwise probe every row only to say `wait: idle` (review, 2026-10-08)
            kind, detail = week_tight(repo)
            if kind == "none":
                return f"skip: no account ({detail})"
            if kind == "tight":
                return f"skip: week tight ({detail})"

        # the last reply, then the idle clock
        sid = owner or queue_session(state) or (trailer.group(1) if trailer else None) \
            or next((e["sessionId"] for e in on), None)
        tr = transcript(sid)
        text = reply if reply is not None else (last_reply(tr) if tr else None)
        if text is None:
            return "skip: last reply unknown" + (f" (session {sid[:8]})" if sid else " (no session)")
        veto = reply_veto(text, live=bool(on))
        if veto:
            return f"skip: {veto}"
        # common.ending, as checkpoint's autofinish_due reads it: link lines may follow the closing line
        # (review at rename S2's /finish — the last line alone disagreed with the Stop hook's own check)
        declared = rule == "C" and common.ending(text)[1] in DECLARED
        # `/autofinish now`, and rule L: this turn's end
        at_once = (rule == "C" and ov.get("now") is True) or bool(live)  # a deploy under C too
        clock = mtime(state / "last-reply")
        if clock is None:
            clock = max([t for t in (mtime(state / "prompts.json"), mtime(tr)) if t] or [0]) or None
        if clock is None:
            clock = float(git(wt, "log", "-1", "--format=%ct", "HEAD") or 0)
        idle = int(max(0, now() - clock) // 60)
        if idle < IDLE_MIN and not declared and not at_once:
            return f"wait: idle {idle} min"

    # GitHub: pushed, not moved on, the PR still WIP and quiet since the tip
    remote = origin_tip(repo, branch)
    if remote is None:
        return "skip: origin unreachable"
    if not remote:
        return "skip: not pushed"
    if remote != tip:
        if git(wt, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", remote, tip) is not None:
            return "skip: not pushed (local is ahead of origin)"
        if not (retry and own_push(wt, run, remote)):
            return "skip: moved on origin"
    since = float(git(wt, "log", "-1", "--format=%ct", "HEAD") or 0)
    gh = github_state(repo, branch, since)
    if gh is None:
        return "skip: GitHub unreachable"
    pr = gh.get("pr")
    if not pr:
        return "skip: no PR"
    if not str(pr.get("title") or "").startswith("WIP:"):
        return f"skip: PR #{pr.get('number')} is no longer WIP"
    if gh.get("activity"):
        return f"skip: GitHub activity since the tip ({gh['activity']} on #{pr.get('number')})"
    return f"start ({rule})"


# ---- repos --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def known_repos():
    """[(name, path)] from repos.json whose own workflow.json says checkpoint: true, or, with
    none on disk yet, whose repos.json entry opts it in (bar a .claude/no-workflow)."""
    try:
        names = list(json.loads(REPOS_JSON.read_text()).get("repos") or {})
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return []
    found = []
    for name in names:
        path = ROOT / name
        try:
            if json.loads((path / ".claude" / "workflow.json").read_text()).get("checkpoint") is True:
                found.append((name, path))
        except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
            if (path.is_dir() and not (path / ".claude" / "no-workflow").exists()
                    and isinstance(common.workflow_overlay(path, REPOS_JSON), dict)):
                found.append((name, path))  # opted in from repos.json, not yet written here
    return found


def resolve(arg):
    """A repos.json key or a path → (name, primary checkout)."""
    for name, path in known_repos():
        if arg == name:
            return name, path
    p = Path(arg).expanduser()
    if not p.is_dir():
        sys.exit(f"autofinish: no repo {arg}")
    top = git(p, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
    primary = Path(top).parent if top else p.resolve()
    for name, path in known_repos():
        if path.resolve() == primary.resolve():
            return name, path
    return primary.name, primary


def dry_run(name, repo):
    """[(branch, verdict, (wt, gitdir, main, cfg))] for every claude/* worktree of one repo; the
    third part is what a start needs to judge the worktree again."""
    main = branches.main_branch(repo)
    cfg = repo_config(repo, main)
    todo = [(Path(wt), branch, Path(gitdir)) for wt, branch, gitdir in branches.worktrees(repo)
            if branch and branch.startswith("claude/") and gitdir is not None]
    # judged JUDGE_THREADS at a time, listed in worktree order as before (map keeps it); judge()
    # never raises, so one odd worktree still cannot hide the rest
    try:
        tier_check()  # its env pop, before any thread copies os.environ (tier_check's comment)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — a verdict that needs it reports the error itself
        pass
    pool = ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=JUDGE_THREADS)
    try:
        verdicts = list(pool.map(lambda t: judge(repo, t[0], t[1], t[2], main, cfg), todo))
    finally:  # a Ctrl-C drops the queued verdicts instead of waiting out each one's git timeouts
        pool.shutdown(wait=True, cancel_futures=True)
    return [(branch, v, (wt, gitdir, main, cfg)) for (wt, branch, gitdir), v in zip(todo, verdicts)]


def judge(repo, wt, branch, gitdir, main, cfg, own=None, reply=None, fetch=False):
    try:
        return verdict(repo, wt, branch, gitdir, main, cfg, own, reply, fetch)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — one odd worktree must not hide the rest
        return f"skip: error ({type(e).__name__}: {str(e)[:80]})"


def stamp():
    return datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(now()).astimezone()


def log_lines(rows, kind="dry"):
    """Append `<iso> <kind> <repo> <branch> <text>` per (repo, branch, text) to the day's log."""
    t = stamp()
    path = LOG_DIR / f"autofinish-{t.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')}.log"
    LOG_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    iso = t.isoformat(timespec="seconds")
    with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        for name, branch, v in rows:
            fh.write(f"{iso} {kind} {name} {branch} {v}\n")
    return path


# ---- this Mac's state: the switch, the tried tips, the sweep lock -------------------------

def armed():
    return common.autofinish_armed()


def keep_first_arm(ts):
    """Write `first-armed` once ({"ts"}): the launch door counts from it, across disarm and re-arm."""
    if ts and first_armed_ts() is None:
        tmp = STATE / f".first-armed.{os.getpid()}"
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps({"ts": ts}) + "\n")
        os.replace(tmp, STATE / "first-armed")


def arm(by):
    STATE.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    ts = stamp().isoformat(timespec="seconds")
    keep_first_arm((armed() or {}).get("ts") or ts)
    tmp = STATE / f".armed.{os.getpid()}"
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps({"by": by, "ts": ts}) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, STATE / "armed")


def disarm():
    a = armed()
    if a:
        keep_first_arm(a.get("ts"))
    try:
        (STATE / "armed").unlink()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass


def switch_line():
    a = armed()
    if not a:
        return "disarmed — verdicts and logs only; nothing starts (autofinish arm)"
    return f"armed by {a.get('by') or '?'} at {a.get('ts') or '?'} — `start` verdicts start runners (autofinish disarm)"


TRIED = "tried.jsonl"


def unattended_refusal(verb):
    """Why `verb` is refused here, or None: a runner, or any dispatch session, never arms the
    launcher nor writes the owner's override (review, 2026-10-05: the marker says `by: <owner>`, and the
    runner's allowlist pre-approves bin/*)."""
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDE_UNATTENDED"):
        return (f"autofinish {verb}: refused in an unattended session (CLAUDE_UNATTENDED="
                f"{os.environ['CLAUDE_UNATTENDED']}) — arming and the override are {OWNER_S}")
    return None


def typed_autofinish(here):
    """The last prompt queued in `here`'s checkout (checkpoint.py's prompts.json, which the
    UserPromptSubmit hook writes before the model sees the prompt) when it is the owner's
    /autofinish, else None (falsy)."""
    gd = git(here, "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir")
    if not gd:
        return None
    try:
        q = json.loads((Path(gd) / "claude" / "prompts.json").read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    last = q[-1] if isinstance(q, list) and q else None
    text = last.get("prompt") if isinstance(last, dict) else last
    return text if isinstance(text, str) and text.lstrip().startswith("/autofinish") else None


def tried(repo, branch, tip):
    """True when a runner was already started on this tip: never again (design section 7)."""
    try:
        lines = (STATE / TRIED).read_text().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return False
    for raw in lines:
        try:
            e = json.loads(raw)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if e.get("tip") == tip and e.get("branch") == branch and e.get("repo") == str(repo):
            return True
    return False


def remember(repo, branch, tip, rule, kind):
    STATE.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with open(STATE / TRIED, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps({"ts": stamp().isoformat(timespec="seconds"), "repo": str(repo),
                             "branch": branch, "tip": tip, "rule": rule, "kind": kind}) + "\n")


def take_lock():
    """True when this process now holds the sweep lock (R12: two sweeps, or a sweep and a
    SessionEnd start, never start two runners at once). mkdir is the atomic test-and-set; a lock
    older than LOCK_TTL is a holder that died, taken over."""
    STATE.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    lock = STATE / "sweep.lock"
    for _ in range(2):
        try:
            lock.mkdir()
            (lock / "pid").write_text(str(os.getpid()))
            return True
        except FileExistsError:
            age = time.time() - (mtime(lock) or time.time())
            if age < LOCK_TTL:
                return False
            for f in lock.iterdir():
                f.unlink()
            lock.rmdir()
    return False


def drop_lock():
    """Only our own: a sweep that outlived LOCK_TTL had its lock taken over, and removing the
    newer holder's would let a third start beside it."""
    lock = STATE / "sweep.lock"
    if lock_holder() != str(os.getpid()):
        return
    try:
        for f in lock.iterdir():
            f.unlink()
        lock.rmdir()
    except OSError:
        pass


def lock_holder():
    try:
        return (STATE / "sweep.lock" / "pid").read_text().strip()
    except OSError:
        return "?"


# ---- the start ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

def run_start(wt, rule, cfg=None):
    """start_runner, or in the tests AUTOFINISH_RUNNER run as `<it> <worktree> <rule>` (a
    non-zero exit is a failed start)."""
    cmd = os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_RUNNER")
    if not cmd:
        return start_runner(wt, rule, cfg)
    env = dict(os.environ)
    if cfg is not None:  # as start_runner passes it, so a test sees the gate's pick
        env["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = cfg or str(Path.home() / ".claude")
    r = subprocess.run([cmd, str(wt), rule], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=RUNNER_TIMEOUT, env=env)
    if r.returncode:
        raise RuntimeError((r.stderr.strip() or f"exit {r.returncode}")[:120])


def restartable(gitdir, tip):
    """The worktree's last run was on this tip and never started (dispatch refused it before any
    session existed — its run cap, its start lock), or died before an outcome (the verdict's
    `died twice` caps that at one retry): bin/autofinish-run starts such a tip again, so a
    remembered start does not hold it back either."""
    rec = common.autofinish_run(Path(gitdir) / "claude") or {}
    return rec.get("tip") == tip and (rec.get("gate") in REFUSED_GATES or rec.get("outcome") == DIED)


def open_steps(wt, base):
    """Entry D6-A (S7): None when no brief the branch added names a step its Course doc has open,
    else the first such line from `keel landing` — a skip, never a start, and nothing written."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([MEMO, "landing", "--base", base], cwd=str(wt), capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=MEMO_TIMEOUT)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        return f"keel landing unavailable: {type(e).__name__}"
    if r.returncode == 0:
        return None
    lines = (r.stdout or r.stderr).strip().splitlines()
    return (lines[0][len("open step: "):] if lines and lines[0].startswith("open step: ") else
            (lines[0] if lines else f"keel landing exit {r.returncode}"))


def stand_docs(wt, docs):
    """Rule D's own step: `memo stand --apply` on each due doc, in the worktree, so the runner's
    /finish finds the Applied replies committed rather than open D-items it must stop on. Raises
    on the first that fails: nothing starts on a half-stood branch."""
    for doc in docs:
        r = subprocess.run([MEMO, "stand", "--apply", doc], cwd=str(wt), capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=MEMO_TIMEOUT)
        if r.returncode:
            raise RuntimeError(f"memo stand --apply {doc}: " + ((r.stderr or r.stdout).strip() or f"exit {r.returncode}"))


def attempt(name, repo, branch, ctx, own=None, reply=None, v=None):
    """Start one worktree whose verdict read `start`: the log kind it came to (start, failed),
    or None when it did not try — a tip started before, or a verdict that changed when judged
    again just now. `v`: the caller's verdict, judged with a fetch seconds ago (one()); without
    it — the sweep, whose dry run is minutes old — it is judged again here with a fetch (R7:
    origin moved since, a prompt arrived, the owner edited a standing doc on GitHub)."""
    global _live
    wt, gitdir, main, cfg = ctx
    tip = git(wt, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
    if tried(repo, branch, tip) and not restartable(gitdir, tip):
        return None
    if v is None:
        hook = os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_TEST_BEFORE_START")
        if hook:
            subprocess.run(hook, shell=True, check=False)
        _remote.pop(str(repo), None)  # origin and the registry read fresh, not the dry run's copies
        _live = None
        _week.clear()
        _picked.clear()
        v = judge(repo, wt, branch, gitdir, main, cfg, own, reply, fetch=True)
        if not v.startswith("start ("):
            log_lines([(name, branch, v)])
            return None
    rule = v[len("start ("):-1]
    # no row may run it: refused before rule D's stand commits (the runner would refuse after it,
    # leaving the branch ahead of origin) — C and D included, which the week never pauses
    kind, detail = week_tight(repo, fresh=True)
    if kind == "none":
        log_lines([(name, branch, f"skip: no account ({detail})")])
        return None
    if kind == "tight" and rule in ("A", "B", "E", "M"):  # judge() read the cache; this is the fresh reading
        log_lines([(name, branch, f"skip: week tight ({detail})")])
        return None
    try:
        if rule == "D":
            stand_docs(wt, _due.get(str(wt), []))
        run_start(wt, rule, _picked.get(str(repo)))
        kind, text = "start", rule
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — a failed start is logged, never a crashed sweep
        kind, text = "failed", f"{rule} ({type(e).__name__}: {str(e)[:100]})"
    if kind == "start":  # a failed start is tried again next tick: nothing ran on that tip
        remember(repo, branch, git(wt, "rev-parse", "HEAD"), rule, kind)
    log_lines([(name, branch, text)], kind)
    print(f"{kind} {name} {branch} {text}", flush=True)
    return kind


def sweep():
    """launchd's entry: every repo judged and logged; armed, at most SWEEP_CAP starts."""
    on = armed()
    if on and not take_lock():
        print(f"autofinish: a sweep or start (pid {lock_holder()}) holds {STATE / 'sweep.lock'}; "
              f"this tick does nothing", flush=True)
        return 0
    try:
        repos = known_repos()
        if on:  # disarmed is verdicts and logs only: its dry run says `died, not recorded yet`
            bury_dead(repos)
        rows, ready = [], []
        for name, repo in repos:
            for branch, v, ctx in dry_run(name, repo):
                rows.append((name, branch, v))
                if v.startswith("start ("):
                    ready.append((name, repo, branch, ctx))
        log_lines(rows)
        if not on:
            return 0
        started = 0
        for name, repo, branch, ctx in ready:
            if started >= SWEEP_CAP:
                break
            if attempt(name, repo, branch, ctx) == "start":
                started += 1
        return 0
    finally:
        if on:
            drop_lock()


# ---- one worktree: SessionEnd, the Stop hook, /autofinish -----------------------------------

def locate(path):
    """(name, repo, branch, (wt, gitdir, main, cfg)) for the checkout holding `path`, or exits."""
    p = Path(path).expanduser()
    top = git(p, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    gitdir = git(p, "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir")
    if not top or not gitdir:
        sys.exit(f"autofinish: {path} is not in a git checkout")
    branch = git(p, "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "--short", "HEAD") or ""
    name, repo = resolve(top)
    main = branches.main_branch(repo)
    return name, repo, branch, (Path(top), Path(gitdir), main, repo_config(repo, main))


def find_worktree(word, here, unborn_ok=False):
    """The worktree of the repo `here` is in whose branch is `word`, or ends in `-<word>` (a
    topic), or exits naming what it found. `unborn_ok`: None for a full claude/* branch name no
    worktree carries yet (the yes at the brief, S7)."""
    name, repo = resolve(here)
    hits = [Path(wt) for wt, branch, _ in branches.worktrees(repo) if branch and branch.startswith("claude/")
            and (branch == word or branch.endswith("-" + word) or branch.endswith("/" + word))]
    if not hits and unborn_ok and word.startswith("claude/"):
        return None
    if len(hits) != 1:
        sys.exit(f"autofinish: {len(hits)} claude/* worktrees of {name} match {word!r}"
                 + ("" if hits else " (git worktree list names them)"))
    return hits[0]


def one(path, after=0, session=None, reply_file=None, dry=False, log=False):
    """Judge one worktree, print its line, and start it when armed and `start`."""
    if after:
        if os.environ.get("AUTOFINISH_NOW"):  # a pinned clock moves; nothing sleeps (the tests)
            os.environ["AUTOFINISH_NOW"] = str(now() + after)
        else:
            time.sleep(after)
    name, repo, branch, ctx = locate(path)
    if not branch.startswith("claude/"):
        print(f"{name} {branch or '(detached)'} skip: not a claude/* worktree")
        return 0
    reply = None
    if reply_file:
        try:
            reply = Path(reply_file).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        except OSError:
            reply = None  # the transcript's, then
    own = own_sessions(session)
    go = not dry and armed()  # a start may follow: judge with a fetch, and only once (F9)
    v = judge(repo, ctx[0], branch, ctx[1], ctx[2], ctx[3], own, reply, fetch=go)
    print(f"{name} {branch} {v}", flush=True)
    if log:
        log_lines([(name, branch, v)])
    if not go or not v.startswith("start ("):
        return 0
    if not take_lock():
        print(f"autofinish: a sweep or start (pid {lock_holder()}) holds the lock; the next sweep looks again")
        return 0
    try:
        attempt(name, repo, branch, ctx, own, reply, v)
    finally:
        drop_lock()
    return 0


def set_override(args, here):
    """`override [now|off] [<branch>]`: /autofinish's marker, then the verdict and the switch."""
    mode = "off" if "off" in args else "now" if "now" in args else "set"
    rest = [a for a in args if a not in ("now", "off")]
    if len(rest) > 1:
        sys.exit("usage: autofinish override [now | off] [<branch>]")
    why = unattended_refusal("override")
    if why:
        sys.exit(why)
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDECODE") and not typed_autofinish(here):
        sys.exit(f"autofinish override: refused — inside a Claude session it runs only for {OWNER_S} typed "
                 f"/autofinish (the last prompt queued in this checkout's prompts.json); ask {OWNER} to type it")
    target = find_worktree(rest[0], here, unborn_ok=True) if rest else Path(here)
    if target is None:
        return set_brief(rest[0], mode, here)
    name, repo, branch, ctx = locate(target)
    if not branch.startswith("claude/"):
        sys.exit(f"autofinish: {name} is on {branch or 'a detached HEAD'} here, not a claude/* worktree — "
                 f"the override is per branch (give one: autofinish override <branch>)")
    marker = ctx[1] / "claude" / common.AUTOFINISH_OVERRIDE
    if mode == "off":
        brief = brief_path(repo, branch)
        if brief and brief.exists():   # a yes at the brief this worktree adopted goes with it
            brief.unlink()
            print(f"the yes at the brief off: {branch}")
        try:
            marker.unlink()
            print(f"override off: {branch} — back to the repo's own start rules")
        except FileNotFoundError:
            print(f"no override on {branch}")
        run = common.autofinish_running(ctx[1] / "claude", common.read_owner(ctx[1] / "claude"))
        if run:  # the marker only gates the next start; a /finish under way ends in its window
            print(f"a run is under way since {common.iso_clock(run.get('started'))} in the window "
                  f"\"{common.autofinish_window(run, branch)}\" — off does not stop it; end it there")
    else:
        marker.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        tmp = marker.parent / f".autofinish.{os.getpid()}"
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps({"by": common.OVERRIDE_BY, "ts": int(now()), "now": mode == "now"}) + "\n")
        os.replace(tmp, marker)
        print(f"override set: {branch} — rule C, "
              + ("at this turn's end (the Stop hook judges it)" if mode == "now"
                 else "after 40 min idle, or at once on the declared line"))
    v = judge(repo, ctx[0], branch, ctx[1], ctx[2], ctx[3], own_sessions())
    print(f"{name} {branch} {v}")
    print(switch_line())
    return 0


def set_brief(branch, mode, here):
    """`override [off] <claude/* branch>` for a branch no worktree carries yet: the yes at the brief
    (S7, D2-C). Its refusals are set_override's, run before this; `now` names a turn's end, which an
    unborn branch has none of."""
    name, repo = resolve(here)
    if mode == "now":
        sys.exit(f"autofinish override now: refused — {branch} has no worktree in {name}, so no turn ends "
                 f"there; give the yes at the brief without `now`")
    if mode != "off":
        # a typo in a full name must not quietly arm a branch that will never exist (landing review,
        # finding 21): the name is the house's whole shape, a worktree carrying its topic is refused as
        # the likelier meaning, and in a session the owner's typed /autofinish names it word for word
        m = BRANCH_RE.match(branch)
        if not m:
            sys.exit(f"autofinish override: refused — {branch!r} names no worktree of {name}, and a branch not yet "
                     f"born must be spelled whole: claude/<host>/<yyyy-mm-dd>-<topic>")
        # the same topic exactly, not one that merely ends in it (…-exclude is no typo of …-autofinish-exclude)
        near = sorted(b for _, b, _ in branches.worktrees(repo) if b and b.startswith("claude/")
                      and BRANCH_RE.match(b) and BRANCH_RE.match(b).group("topic") == m.group("topic"))
        if near:
            sys.exit(f"autofinish override: refused — {branch} has no worktree in {name}, but {near[0]} carries its "
                     f"topic; give that branch (a typo?), or a new topic for a new branch")
        typed = typed_autofinish(here) if os.environ.get("CLAUDECODE") else None
        if os.environ.get("CLAUDECODE") and branch not in (typed or "").split():
            sys.exit(f"autofinish override: refused — the yes at the brief names its branch in {OWNER_S} typed "
                     f"/autofinish itself, and the last prompt queued does not name {branch}")
    path = brief_path(repo, branch)
    if not path:
        sys.exit(f"autofinish: {name} has no git dir here")
    if mode == "off":
        try:
            path.unlink()
            print(f"the yes at the brief off: {branch}")
        except FileNotFoundError:
            print(f"no yes at the brief for {branch}")
        return 0
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path.parent / f".brief.{os.getpid()}"
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps({"by": common.OVERRIDE_BY, "ts": int(now()), "branch": branch, "brief": True}) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, path)
    print(f"the yes at the brief set: {branch} in {name} — rule C once a worktree carries it, after 40 min "
          f"idle or at once on the declared line; it lands class 1 and 3, and holds class 2, in_place and "
          f"the list at a ready PR")
    print(switch_line())
    return 0


def main(argv=None):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="autofinish", description=__doc__.split("\n")[0])
    ap.add_argument("cmd", nargs="?", choices=("arm", "disarm", "status", "override"))
    ap.add_argument("args", nargs="*", help="override: [now | off] [<branch>]")
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", nargs="?", const="", metavar="REPO",
                    help="one verdict per claude/* worktree (every workflow repo when no REPO)")
    ap.add_argument("--log", action="store_true", help="append the verdicts to the day's log")
    ap.add_argument("--sweep", action="store_true", help="launchd's tick: judge, log, and (armed) start")
    ap.add_argument("--worktree", metavar="PATH", help="judge one worktree, and (armed) start it")
    ap.add_argument("--after", type=float, default=0, metavar="S", help="with --worktree: wait first")
    ap.add_argument("--session", metavar="ID", help="with --worktree: the caller's session, never busy for itself")
    ap.add_argument("--reply", metavar="FILE", help="with --worktree: the last reply's text, in place of the transcript")
    ap.add_argument("--by", default=os.environ.get("USER") or "?", help="arm: who armed it")
    a = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if a.cmd == "arm":
        why = unattended_refusal("arm")
        if why:
            sys.exit(why)
        arm(a.by)
        print(switch_line())
        return 0
    if a.cmd == "disarm":
        disarm()
        print(switch_line())
        return 0
    if a.cmd == "status":
        print(switch_line())
        return 0
    if a.cmd == "override":
        return set_override(a.args, Path.cwd())
    if a.sweep:
        return sweep()
    if a.worktree:
        if a.dry_run:
            ap.error("--worktree takes --dry-run without a repo")
        return one(a.worktree, a.after, a.session, a.reply, dry=a.dry_run is not None, log=a.log)
    if a.dry_run is None:
        ap.error("give --dry-run, --sweep, --worktree, or arm | disarm | status | override")
    targets = [resolve(a.dry_run)] if a.dry_run else known_repos()
    rows = []
    for name, repo in targets:
        for branch, v, _ in dry_run(name, repo):
            rows.append((name, branch, v))
            print(f"{name} {branch} {v}", flush=True)
    if a.log:
        log_lines(rows)
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
